{"trace_id": "real-01", "origin": "real", "question": "What are the four main approaches to evaluating LLMs?", "retrieved": [{"passage_id": "c472c377-46e7-4e1d-8908-93e0d1545bfe", "title": "Understanding the 4 Main Approaches to LLM Evaluation (From Scratch)", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/llm-evaluation-4-approaches", "published_at": "2025-10-05T11:12:32+00:00", "text": "happening on the LLM research front right now. I’m still catching up on my growing list of bookmarked papers and plan to highlight some of the most interesting ones in the next article.\nBut now, let’s discuss the four main LLM evaluation methods along with their from-scratch code implementations to better understand their advantages and weaknesses.\nUnderstanding the main evaluation methods for LLMs\nThere are four common ways of evaluating trained LLMs in practice: multiple choice, verifiers, leaderboards, and LLM judges, as shown in Figure 1 below. Research papers, marketing materials, technical reports, and model cards (a term for LLM-specific technical reports) often include results from two or more of these categories.\nFurthermore the four categories introduced here fall into two groups: benchmark-based evaluation and judgment-based evaluation, as shown in the figure above.\n(There are also other measures, such as training loss, perplexity, and rewards, but they are usually used", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "4fe04b44-f00f-47d1-b161-7e9a3e9c7bd7", "title": "Understanding the 4 Main Approaches to LLM Evaluation (From Scratch)", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/llm-evaluation-4-approaches", "published_at": "2025-10-05T11:12:32+00:00", "text": "be biased by model preferences, prompt design, and answer style. Also, there is a strong dependency on the choice of judge model and rubric, and they lack the reproducibility of fixed benchmarks.\nConclusion\nIn this article, we covered four different evaluation approaches: multiple choice, verifiers, leaderboards, and LLM judges.\nI know this was a long article, but I hope you found it useful for getting an overview of how LLMs are evaluated. A from-scratch approach like this can be verbose, but it is a great way to understand how these methods work under the hood, which in turn helps us identify weaknesses and areas for improvement.\nThat being said, you are probably wondering, “What is the best way to evaluate an LLM?” Unfortunately, there is no single best method since, as we have seen, each comes with different trade-offs. In short:\nMultiple-choice\n(+) Relatively quick and cheap to run at scale\n(+) Standardized and reproducible across papers (or model cards)\n(-) Measures basic", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "e989afe1-ff5e-4191-ad75-19c853506d25", "title": "Understanding the 4 Main Approaches to LLM Evaluation (From Scratch)", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/llm-evaluation-4-approaches", "published_at": "2025-10-05T11:12:32+00:00", "text": "How do we actually evaluate LLMs?\nIt’s a simple question, but one that tends to open up a much bigger discussion.\nWhen advising or collaborating on projects, one of the things I get asked most often is how to choose between different models and how to make sense of the evaluation results out there. (And, of course, how to measure progress when fine-tuning or developing our own.)\nSince this comes up so often, I thought it might be helpful to share a short overview of the main evaluation methods people use to compare LLMs. Of course, LLM evaluation is a very big topic that can’t be exhaustively covered in a single resource, but I think that having a clear mental map of these main approaches makes it much easier to interpret benchmarks, leaderboards, and papers.\nI originally planned to include these evaluation techniques in my upcoming book, Build a Reasoning Model (From Scratch), but they ended up being a bit outside the main scope. (The book itself focuses more on verifier-based", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "2b0ad521-24c6-4f47-a5d0-3effb9c83007", "title": "The State Of LLMs 2025: Progress, Problems, and Predictions", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/state-of-llms-2025", "published_at": "2025-12-30T12:22:26+00:00", "text": "if it scores above X on benchmark Y, that doesn’t imply it’s much better than another LLM that scores above X on the same benchmark.\nAnother aspect to consider is that image classifiers have only one job, namely, classifying images. However, LLMs are used for many different tasks: translating text, summarizing text, writing code, brainstorming, solving math problems, and many more. Evaluating image classifiers, where a clear metric such as classification accuracy is available, is much simpler than evaluating LLMs on both deterministic and free-form tasks.\nBesides trying out LLMs in practice and constantly generating new benchmarks, there’s unfortunately no solution to this problem.\nBy the way, if you are curious to learn more about the main categories of LLM evaluation, you might like my article Understanding the 4 Main Approaches to LLM Evaluation (From Scratch):\n6. AI for Coding, Writing, and Research\nSince it comes up so often, I wanted to share my two cents about LLM replacing", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "2b5332bc-f3b5-40f4-912f-c2ac6abcf6e0", "title": "Understanding the 4 Main Approaches to LLM Evaluation (From Scratch)", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/llm-evaluation-4-approaches", "published_at": "2025-10-05T11:12:32+00:00", "text": "comparisons as it involves human feedback (as well as the challenges that are associated with collecting this feedback).\nA related method is to use another LLM with a pre-defined grading rubric (i.e., an evaluation guide) to compare an LLM’s response to a reference response and judge the response quality based on a pre-defined rubric, as illustrated in Figure 12.\nIn practice, the judge-based approach shown in Figure 12 works well when the judge LLM is strong. Common setups use leading proprietary LLMs via an API (e.g., the GPT-5 API), though specialized judge models also exist. (E.g., one of the many examples is Phudge; ultimately, most of these specialized models are just smaller models fine-tuned to have similar scoring behavior as proprietary GPT models.)\nOne of the reasons why judges work so well is also that evaluating an answer is often easier than generating one.\nTo implement a judge-based model evaluation as shown in Figue 12 programmatically in Python, we could either load", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}], "citations": [{"url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/llm-evaluation-4-approaches", "title": "Understanding the 4 Main Approaches to LLM Evaluation (From Scratch)", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "passage_id": "c472c377-46e7-4e1d-8908-93e0d1545bfe", "citation_idx": 1}, {"url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/llm-evaluation-4-approaches", "title": "Understanding the 4 Main Approaches to LLM Evaluation (From Scratch)", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "passage_id": "4fe04b44-f00f-47d1-b161-7e9a3e9c7bd7", "citation_idx": 2}, {"url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/llm-evaluation-4-approaches", "title": "Understanding the 4 Main Approaches to LLM Evaluation (From Scratch)", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "passage_id": "2b5332bc-f3b5-40f4-912f-c2ac6abcf6e0", "citation_idx": 5}], "answer_text": "Sebastian Raschka names four common ways of evaluating trained LLMs: **multiple choice**, **verifiers**, **leaderboards**, and **LLM judges** [1][2]. He sorts them into two groups, benchmark-based evaluation and judgment-based evaluation [1]. Papers, technical reports, and model cards often report results from two or more of these categories [1].\n\nHe says no single method is best, because each involves different trade-offs [2]:\n\n- **Multiple choice** is relatively quick and cheap to run at scale, and it is standardized and reproducible [2].\n- **LLM judges** compare a response to a reference answer using a predefined rubric. They work well when the judge model is strong, partly because evaluating an answer is often easier than generating one [5]. However, judges can be biased by model preferences, prompt design, and answer style. Their results also depend heavily on which judge model and rubric are chosen, and they are less reproducible than fixed benchmarks [2].\n\nThe passages do not describe the strengths and weaknesses of verifiers and leaderboards. Raschka also mentions other measures, such as training loss, perplexity, and rewards, which sit outside these four categories [1].", "expected_verdict": "PASS", "failure_code": "NONE", "note": "Every statement matches [1], [2] or [5]; says plainly what the passages do not cover.", "expected": {"deterministic": "PASS", "faithfulness": "PASS", "disagreement": null}}
{"trace_id": "real-02", "origin": "real", "question": "Why does Nathan Lambert not buy into recursive self-improvement?", "retrieved": [{"passage_id": "e2ee12ac-29f9-4c63-8a99-659fa310480e", "title": "Why I still haven’t bought into true RSI", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/where-i-stand-on-rsi", "published_at": "2026-09-19T15:42:20+00:00", "text": "is that a similar thing will happen: they’ll turn out to be directionally correct (relative to the expectations of almost anyone not linked to the community) but factually wrong. Specifically, we won’t have superintelligence within the next 8 years, but things will still be moving so fast that it’ll *feel* like the people who argued for short timelines were right.\n… I wanted to say something now because it feels like the level of bandwagoning towards “singularity soon” is getting pretty wild.\nPersonally, I think this view aligns closely to what I outlined in my alternate scenario to true recursive self-improvement (RSI), which I called lossy self-improvement. A summary of this view is that:\n- Automatable research is too narrow to achieve a massive net acceleration in progress, in the face of scaling laws’ exponential costs,\n- Diminishing returns of more AI agents in parallel are real, &\n- Resource bottlenecks and politics are a major factor in building strong LLMs (and AI can do much", "claim_id": "d9eb5a22-8f4f-5c38-8b29-b3b491afac03", "claim_text": "Nathan Lambert argues that automatable AI research is too narrow to produce massive net acceleration in progress given the exponential costs implied by scaling laws, supporting a 'lossy self-improvement' view over true recursive self-improvement.", "claim_type": "opinion", "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "a848ced4-4883-422e-a073-22e6dd45a49f", "title": "One resignation turned the embers of AI fear into a wildfire", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/one-resignation-turned-the-embers", "published_at": "2026-09-10T15:28:52+00:00", "text": "heavily dependent on AI tools, the current AI tools are superhuman in some domains (e.g. math) – so, all together, AI is going to work more on itself and become superhuman in all relevant areas over time to autonomy and intellect. This view dramatically undersells human bottlenecks in building models and allocating resources at organizations, and draws conclusions on future AI capabilities more broadly.\nI called my alternative view to this, Lossy self-improvement. AI has always been very jagged, and we are making models which are superhuman goal-seekers at math and software engineering, but they have massive limitations on intuitions, creativity, and other types of reasoning that humans are strong at. With AI agents assisting research, we will rapidly find the areas where AI is superhuman – and I expect there to be well more than just research mathematics – but it won’t be a panacea for the current limitations of our approaches to LLMs.\nThere is another understandable social dynamic", "claim_id": "0f4fcd5b-686f-587b-80f0-cc541ffbebb8", "claim_text": "The argument that recursive self-improvement (RSI) will drive runaway AI progress dramatically undersells human bottlenecks in building models and allocating organizational resources.", "claim_type": "opinion", "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "3399b4a0-d7a1-427b-a064-c2dfcdee8afc", "title": "[AINews] GPT-6.2 Astra, the #2 Frontier Model is better and 20%-600% cheaper, but has mixed reviews; 6.2 Sol likely main workhorse", "author": "swyx .ai", "url": "https://latent.space/p/ainews-gpt-6-2-astra-the-2-frontier-model-is-better-and-20-600-cheaper-but-has-mixed-reviews-6-2-sol/", "published_at": "2026-11-18T10:19:04+00:00", "text": "- Astra for Law. The Enterprise configuration adds a legal search index. Artificial Analysis measured 20.8% on Harvey LAB-AA, versus 15.0% for base Astra. Artificial Analysis\n- Preparedness. OpenAI rated Astra High for AI Self-Improvement, a first for one of its externally released models. On a separate measure, Vals’ RSI-Index rose from 27.07% to 29.01%, with 50% denoting its human reference. System card · Vals\n- FrontierMath Erdős. Epoch verified seven solutions in Lean out of 68 problems. The previous best was three. Epoch\nWhat the benchmarks measure\n- Agents’ Last Exam 1.1. OpenAI reports Astra at xhigh gained 5.2 points over GPT-6 Astra at max, using less than a third of the cost per task. It reports a 5.5-point lead over Fable 5.2 at about 5% of the cost on that test. On GDPval v2, it says Astra used 22% fewer output tokens than its predecessor. OpenAI’s results\n- Long engineering runs. Mercor’s OpenWorldEngineer puts Astra at 67.2%, Fable 5.2 at 54.6%, and GPT-6 Astra at 44.2%.", "claim_id": "ec24ddff-a0c3-508b-a44a-1960ad690d60", "claim_text": "OpenAI rated GPT-6.2 Astra High for AI Self-Improvement in its Preparedness framework, the first externally released OpenAI model to get that rating.", "claim_type": "measurement", "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "b2534fc1-c9bf-47e6-8564-e46880f63540", "title": "[AINews] Jensen Huang resigns after 33 years leading NVIDIA", "author": "swyx .ai", "url": "https://latent.space/p/ainews-jensen-huang-resigns-after-33-years-leading-nvidia/", "published_at": "2026-11-13T09:04:13+00:00", "text": "problem (Jason Wei).\n- Training and distillation methods: \n  - Speculative Self-Distillation: The teacher takes over only when the student drifts. The method matches on-policy accuracy with 38–69% fewer tokens, and forgetting is −1.4 points versus −4.5 for on-policy (Shayan Talaei).\n  - SteerDistill: W4A16 agents stay within 1 point of BF16 across 3 models and 5 benchmarks (HF Papers).\n  - Recursive self-improvement in pretraining: Agent loops applied to a full pretraining run produced 801-30B-A3B. The authors say it beats Qwen3-30B-A3B (dair_ai).\n- Agent memory and self-improvement: \n  - SoMem: Agents organize memory as linked notes, gaining +2.66 points on LoCoMo and +3.10 on BEAM-100K (omarsar0).\n  - TRUST: Statistical gating cuts bad harness updates from 45.59% to 2.78% (dair_ai).\n  - Interaction-as-a-Judge: Interaction-based feedback fixes 75.5% of broken requirements versus 55.4% for code-only feedback. Arena also released two datasets (Arena).\n- Eval integrity: \n  -", "claim_id": "9ed3d5a0-bd99-5f38-99c5-52c7a4e0d5e4", "claim_text": "Speculative Self-Distillation, where the teacher intervenes only when the student drifts, matches on-policy distillation accuracy with 38–69% fewer tokens and less forgetting.", "claim_type": "measurement", "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "f0abf6f6-dea4-4d91-8c14-7796c9c0f1e9", "title": "Why I still haven’t bought into true RSI", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/where-i-stand-on-rsi", "published_at": "2026-09-19T15:42:20+00:00", "text": "but we do not yet see clear signs of dramatic acceleration beyond that rate.\nAltogether, I think the hardest exponential we are fighting is on peak intelligence. That is the hardest one to budge or even accelerate. Still, my mental model for the very early innings of RSI is more of massively scaling and diffusing inference-time compute to AI research and related activities, which has a large amount of low-hanging fruit available. This, on its own, is still poised to be economically transformative. It may also unlock more resources to push on AI diffusion, which is the crucial bottleneck in unlocking much of the potential benefits of AI.\nFor now and until more evidence emerges, lossy self-improvement remains my baseline on the trajectory of progress, and the increased discussion of extinction risk seems very misplaced. As always, things can change fast in AI.", "claim_id": "aa9fba7e-115d-5a74-a2e8-324bbb56c97a", "claim_text": "Lambert considers the increased discussion of AI extinction risk very misplaced, holding lossy self-improvement as his baseline for AI progress.", "claim_type": "opinion", "in_prompt": true}], "citations": [{"url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/where-i-stand-on-rsi", "title": "Why I still haven’t bought into true RSI", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "claim_id": "d9eb5a22-8f4f-5c38-8b29-b3b491afac03", "claim_type": "opinion", "passage_id": "e2ee12ac-29f9-4c63-8a99-659fa310480e", "citation_idx": 1}, {"url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/one-resignation-turned-the-embers", "title": "One resignation turned the embers of AI fear into a wildfire", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "claim_id": "0f4fcd5b-686f-587b-80f0-cc541ffbebb8", "claim_type": "opinion", "passage_id": "a848ced4-4883-422e-a073-22e6dd45a49f", "citation_idx": 2}, {"url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/where-i-stand-on-rsi", "title": "Why I still haven’t bought into true RSI", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "claim_id": "aa9fba7e-115d-5a74-a2e8-324bbb56c97a", "claim_type": "opinion", "passage_id": "f0abf6f6-dea4-4d91-8c14-7796c9c0f1e9", "citation_idx": 5}], "answer_text": "Nathan Lambert prefers what he calls \"lossy self-improvement\" to true recursive self-improvement (RSI). His reasons are opinions, not measured findings:\n\n- **Automation is too narrow.** He argues the AI research that can be automated is too narrow to produce a massive net acceleration, because scaling laws imply exponential costs [1].\n- **Parallel agents hit diminishing returns.** Running more AI agents in parallel gives diminishing returns [1].\n- **Humans and resources are bottlenecks.** He says the RSI argument \"dramatically undersells human bottlenecks in building models and allocating resources at organizations,\" and that resource bottlenecks and politics matter a lot in building strong LLMs [1][2].\n- **AI is jagged.** Models are becoming superhuman goal-seekers in areas like math and software engineering, but they have large limits on intuition, creativity and other kinds of reasoning. Using AI agents in research will reveal where AI is superhuman, but it \"won't be a panacea\" for current LLM limitations [2].\n- **No sign of dramatic acceleration yet.** He sees no clear evidence of acceleration beyond the current rate, and thinks peak intelligence is the hardest exponential to move [5].\n\nHe does expect early RSI-like gains from massively scaling inference-time compute on AI research. He thinks that could be economically transformative even so [5]. He also predicts superintelligence will not arrive within eight years, though progress may feel fast enough that short-timeline advocates seem right [1]. Given all this, he finds the growing discussion of extinction risk \"very misplaced,\" while noting things can change fast [5].", "expected_verdict": "PASS", "failure_code": "NONE", "note": "Faithful to [1], [2], [5] and marks the views as opinions. The eight-year forecast in [1] is a view Lambert quotes and says aligns with his own; attributing it to him is acceptable. Two authors in the prompt, no conflict between them.", "expected": {"deterministic": "PASS", "faithfulness": "PASS", "disagreement": "PASS"}}
{"trace_id": "real-03", "origin": "real", "question": "What share of issues in Linear are created by agents, and how did that come about?", "retrieved": [{"passage_id": "ef2bfc5b-2320-44ae-8171-db5be909cb98", "title": "Scaling Linear’s GTM to $100M ARR - Cristina Cordova, COO", "author": "Basil Chatha", "url": "https://latent.space/p/linear-scaling/", "published_at": "2026-11-19T23:58:21+00:00", "text": "were basically the first company in our space to do it. And now, over 50% of issues created on Linear are created by agents in just like a year and a half.\n21:00 Yeah. The work is created in an entirely different way than it was before. You know? So that kind of illustrates how much has changed in a pretty short period of time. And then when we look at how many users are coming to the app and integrating or creating issues within the app itself, that's also going up significantly. Okay. Right? So sometimes people believe that like, oh, I'm just I'm just using MCP server for everything now. But like you are also going back to the app and you're interacting with it because you need to visualize things. And often, like, a chat interface isn't the best way to visualize all the work that a team is doing. It's not great for collaboration because it's typically, like, single player only. Mhmm. And so I think for us, as long as, like, you know, the MCP usage line and the app usage line are", "claim_id": "f204bf9c-abb7-5e59-980c-e1ba4ff0d9f2", "claim_text": "More than half of the issues created in Linear are now created by agents, about a year and a half after Linear launched its MCP server in 2025.", "claim_type": "measurement", "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "b34457ae-a352-49e2-9f77-3bb9f339e526", "title": "Scaling Linear’s GTM to $100M ARR - Cristina Cordova, COO", "author": "Basil Chatha", "url": "https://latent.space/p/linear-scaling/", "published_at": "2026-11-19T23:58:21+00:00", "text": "own Linear Agent. Agents created 3% of Linear's work a year ago. Now it's more than half. Linear passed $100 million in ARR this year with more than 40,000 paying customers and 177% net revenue retention, and in August employees sold $99 million in stock at a $2.5 billion valuation.\nWe talk about why she stopped chasing enterprise deals three weeks after joining, why Linear has never hired a single SDR, why it won't let an LLM answer support tickets on its own, and what the AI budgets behind $100M-in-months ARR mean for everyone else.\nHalf the work on Linear is now created by agents\nLinear shipped its MCP server in May 2025, and Cristina says it was basically the first in its category. A year and a half later, more than half the issues created in Linear come from agents.\nThat doesn't mean Linear wants to be a database with an MCP server on top. Cristina thinks SaaS companies settling for that are making a big mistake. If the agent workflows inside your product aren't better than the", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "397285f5-fe10-4b8a-8809-aeb9c528dad2", "title": "Scaling Linear’s GTM to $100M ARR - Cristina Cordova, COO", "author": "Basil Chatha", "url": "https://latent.space/p/linear-scaling/", "published_at": "2026-11-19T23:58:21+00:00", "text": "Linear was founded to build a better issue tracker. Seven years and $134 million in funding later, its CEO Karri Saarinen published a post titled \"Issue tracking is dead.\" In this episode, we chat with Cristina Cordova, COO of Linear, about how the company navigated that and how its go-to-market changed along the way.\nCristina spent seven and a half years at Stripe, led platform and partnerships at Notion, and was a partner at First Round before joining Linear in 2023. Linear's founders built it after living with legacy issue trackers at Coinbase, Airbnb and Uber, and it spread self-serve through startups like Vercel and Ramp.\nWhen she joined, Linear had about 40 people and one salesperson. Since then it has shipped an MCP server, a platform for building agents into Linear, integrations with Codex, Cursor and GitHub Copilot, and its own Linear Agent. Agents created 3% of Linear's work a year ago. Now it's more than half. Linear passed $100 million in ARR this year with more than", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "1f467424-a6bf-402a-8c71-3e05fa51ecf3", "title": "Scaling Linear’s GTM to $100M ARR - Cristina Cordova, COO", "author": "Basil Chatha", "url": "https://latent.space/p/linear-scaling/", "published_at": "2026-11-19T23:58:21+00:00", "text": "on top. Cristina thinks SaaS companies settling for that are making a big mistake. If the agent workflows inside your product aren't better than the ones outside it, nobody has a reason to open your app.\nSo she watches two lines: agent usage and app usage. If agent usage climbs and app usage doesn't, you're becoming a commodity. At Linear, both are up and to the right, because people still come back to the app to see and coordinate the work.\n\"Issue tracking is dead\"\nWhen Karri published \"Issue tracking is dead\" in March, most of Linear's customers agreed with him. Then they asked the obvious question: so what am I buying? Linear's answer is a product development system built around agent orchestration, where humans stop doing the manual work of filing, labeling, assigning and closing issues.\nCustomers had been pushing it there. Most of the frontier AI labs run on Linear and wanted it to move faster. Pre-AI companies like Ramp and Coinbase were building their own internal agents and", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "dd167330-5d19-4aec-aed2-50e41d975162", "title": "Scaling Linear’s GTM to $100M ARR - Cristina Cordova, COO", "author": "Basil Chatha", "url": "https://latent.space/p/linear-scaling/", "published_at": "2026-11-19T23:58:21+00:00", "text": "created, like, that's like a human going into the app, like our mobile app, our desktop app, you know, our web app, all of those places, and creating work. Right? If you look at how the work is like moving through the pipeline in a way, like getting assigned to an agent, sometimes that's done manually by a human. You know, sometimes an agent does it. Mhmm. But assign... Like delegates to another agent. Right? Yeah. Often, we're now part of the workflow. Right? You can use a feature we have called loops that will, like, automatically, assign an agent to a given issue and have the agent start, like, coding against it, for so in a lot of cases, it's like an agent is doing the work, but it's a Linear agent. Yeah. Right? Which we also wanna see. Right? So we have to look at, like, multiple different factors. But it's generally like app sessions that we're looking at in terms of trying to understand activity. Yeah. So I guess that leads me to like Karri writing this blog post like issue", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}], "citations": [{"url": "https://latent.space/p/linear-scaling/", "title": "Scaling Linear’s GTM to $100M ARR - Cristina Cordova, COO", "author": "Basil Chatha", "claim_id": "f204bf9c-abb7-5e59-980c-e1ba4ff0d9f2", "claim_type": "measurement", "passage_id": "ef2bfc5b-2320-44ae-8171-db5be909cb98", "citation_idx": 1}, {"url": "https://latent.space/p/linear-scaling/", "title": "Scaling Linear’s GTM to $100M ARR - Cristina Cordova, COO", "author": "Basil Chatha", "passage_id": "b34457ae-a352-49e2-9f77-3bb9f339e526", "citation_idx": 2}, {"url": "https://latent.space/p/linear-scaling/", "title": "Scaling Linear’s GTM to $100M ARR - Cristina Cordova, COO", "author": "Basil Chatha", "passage_id": "397285f5-fe10-4b8a-8809-aeb9c528dad2", "citation_idx": 3}, {"url": "https://latent.space/p/linear-scaling/", "title": "Scaling Linear’s GTM to $100M ARR - Cristina Cordova, COO", "author": "Basil Chatha", "passage_id": "1f467424-a6bf-402a-8c71-3e05fa51ecf3", "citation_idx": 4}, {"url": "https://latent.space/p/linear-scaling/", "title": "Scaling Linear’s GTM to $100M ARR - Cristina Cordova, COO", "author": "Basil Chatha", "passage_id": "dd167330-5d19-4aec-aed2-50e41d975162", "citation_idx": 5}], "answer_text": "More than half of the issues created in Linear now come from agents, up from 3% a year earlier [1][2][3]. Cristina Cordova, Linear's COO, reports this as a measured figure [1].\n\n**How it came about:**\n- **MCP server:** Linear shipped an MCP server in May 2025. Cordova says Linear was basically the first company in its category to do so [1][2]. The over-50% share was reached about a year and a half after that launch [1][2].\n- **Agent tooling:** Linear also built a platform for building agents into Linear, integrations with Codex, Cursor and GitHub Copilot, and its own Linear Agent [3]. A feature called \"loops\" can automatically assign an agent to an issue and have it start coding against it [5].\n- **Customer pressure:** Most frontier AI labs run on Linear and wanted it to move faster. Pre-AI companies such as Ramp and Coinbase were building their own internal agents [4].\n- **Strategic shift:** CEO Karri Saarinen's post \"Issue tracking is dead\" recast Linear as a product development system built around agent orchestration. In that system, humans no longer do the manual work of filing, labeling, assigning and closing issues [4].\n\nApp usage has also risen alongside agent usage. Cordova watches both numbers, and says that if only agent usage grew, Linear would risk becoming a commodity [1][4].", "expected_verdict": "PASS", "failure_code": "NONE", "note": "All figures (over half, 3% a year earlier, May 2025) are in the cited passages.", "expected": {"deterministic": "PASS", "faithfulness": "PASS", "disagreement": null}}
{"trace_id": "real-04", "origin": "real", "question": "How do Kubernetes ingress controllers route traffic to services?", "retrieved": [{"passage_id": "4557b4d0-daaf-44fa-b9c3-24b7eca97ec1", "title": "Open-Source AI & Open Models Reading List", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/open-source-ai-reading-list", "published_at": "2026-09-11T12:36:25+00:00", "text": "is the wrong approach to AI misuse, Helen Toner (Apr. 2025)\n- Distillation – the process of training on output tokens from another model – is the single most eventful debate around open models in 2026. \n  - For basic background, see a textbook chapter on synthetic data & distillation generally, from Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (post-training textbook published in 2026)\n  - How distillation helps the Chinese labs, but doesn’t take away from their innovation - How much does distillation really matter for Chinese LLMs?, Nathan Lambert / Interconnects (Feb. 2026)\n  - A very transparent documentation of how Chinese company use Anthropic’s products and circumvent the terms of service or intended use. The report details at-scale usage of Anthropic’s products by banned parties, as a mix of technical distillation (mentioned via SFT data) and extensive routing of Claude into their products and services without telling users -Detecting and countering misuse of AI: September 2026.", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "bd7f2d9b-004f-4600-b198-832075775ab2", "title": "GPT-6 Astra, Looped Transformers, and Hidden Reasoning", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/gpt-6-astra-looped-transformers-and", "published_at": "2026-09-09T11:14:25+00:00", "text": "approach here uses a small, learned router. This is similar to the routing idea in a mixture-of-experts model, except that here the routing decision determines how many times to apply the shared stack.\nThe router operates on a token’s hidden representation, which also contains information about its context. So, we shouldn’t think of this as assigning every occurrence of a particular token the same number of passes (i.e., the word “People” in the figure above doesn’t always go through a loop of 3). The decision can change depending on where that word appears and what came before it.\nNow, how does the routing work exactly? The paper explores two ways to make this routing decision, as illustrated below.\nIn expert-choice routing, which is shown in the left subpanel in the figure above, each recursion step selects which tokens it will process. Tokens that exit are excluded from later steps. In token-choice routing, shown on the right, the router makes one decision at the beginning,", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "966a2a23-5373-47c9-a02d-3c995d40669e", "title": "GPT-6 Astra, Looped Transformers, and Hidden Reasoning", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/gpt-6-astra-looped-transformers-and", "published_at": "2026-09-09T11:14:25+00:00", "text": "process. Tokens that exit are excluded from later steps. In token-choice routing, shown on the right, the router makes one decision at the beginning, assigning each token to a path with one, two, or three passes.\nIn both cases, the transformer weights are reused across passes, similar to Nanbeige, etc. But the additional flexibility comes from choosing how much computation each token receives. The model and its routers are trained together, so the model learns to work with these different paths during training.\n2.5 How well does this work?\nThe plot from the Mixture-of-Recursions paper below compares a regular transformer (Vanilla), a transformer with fixed recursion (Recursive), and Mixture-of-Recursions (MoR) for different model sizes and compute budgets (x-axis).\nAt the smallest model scale, the regular transformer performs best. For the larger models, Mixture-of-Recursions catches up and often performs better, especially at the smaller training budgets. At the largest budget,", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "e2ba7b93-3a64-477e-baf4-2846c2765c73", "title": "Open-Source AI & Open Models Reading List", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/open-source-ai-reading-list", "published_at": "2026-09-11T12:36:25+00:00", "text": "data) and extensive routing of Claude into their products and services without telling users -Detecting and countering misuse of AI: September 2026.\n  - A recent paper that showed that the frontier labs had implementations in their APIs that made systematic extraction of reasoning traces (the crucial part of modern training) through clever tricks. Recent distillation paper, my writing on it - Stealing Reasoning Traces from Proprietary LLM APIs, Panfilov, Schmotz, Shumailov et. al 2026 (more on X). Anthropic confirmed this technique was used by Chinese labs.\n  - Why the political panic over distillation, claiming that distillation is the only reason Chinese models are close to the frontier, is not grounded in the evidence - The distillation panic, Nathan Lambert / Interconnects (May 2026)\n  - How labs can use distillation to improve models in an era of scaling RL environments across agentic behaviors - How distillation is used today and what performance uplift it gives to open models,", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "1d257d3a-aeb9-47a3-aeeb-bb4cddd794cc", "title": "Was I wrong about open models & cyber risks?", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/was-i-wrong-about-open-models-and", "published_at": "2026-10-30T17:36:56+00:00", "text": "diffusion is the bottleneck on cyber attacks (it would look like a step change):\nWhen talking to people who are close to the AI community, especially AI safety, I find a lot of effort going into figuring out if open models are to blame, which we still have extremely low evidence for. Closed models have played a very heavy part in the recent hacks, at least more than open models have. Cyber is really a societal problem, one which AI companies alone cannot solve. Effort needs to be spent at this higher level of abstraction. This is the first of many risks and transitions we’ll need to manage with AI, none of which can be solved solely by the San Francisco centric tech industry.\nThanks to Seb Krier for feedback on this post.\nIf you’re interested in sharing Interconnects with your team, I offer tiered discounts for group subscriptions. Please reach out if you’re interested!", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}], "citations": [], "answer_text": "The retrieved passages do not cover Kubernetes ingress controllers or how they route traffic to services; they discuss \"routing\" only for token routing in Mixture-of-Recursions models and for routing Claude into products in the AI-distillation debate.", "expected_verdict": "PASS", "failure_code": "NONE", "note": "Nothing retrieved addresses the question and the answer says so instead of answering from memory.", "expected": {"deterministic": "PASS", "faithfulness": "PASS", "disagreement": "PASS"}}
{"trace_id": "real-05", "origin": "real", "question": "What does Sebastian Raschka predict about where LLM progress will come from in 2026?", "retrieved": [{"passage_id": "28f65a58-8df4-4357-957d-d9e6b21e40dd", "title": "The State Of LLMs 2025: Progress, Problems, and Predictions", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/state-of-llms-2025", "published_at": "2025-12-30T12:22:26+00:00", "text": "the Linux Foundation) has already become the standard for tool and data access in agent-style LLM systems (for now); I expected the ecosystem to remain more fragmented in 2025, until at least 2026.\n9.2 Predictions for 2026\n- We will likely see an industry-scale, consumer-facing diffusion model for cheap, reliable, low-latency inference, with Gemini Diffusion probably going first.\n- The open-weight community will slowly but steadily adopt LLMs with local tool use and increasingly agentic capabilities.\n- RLVR will more widely expand into other domains beyond math and coding (for example, chemistry, biology, and others).\n- Classical RAG will slowly fade as a default solution for document queries. Instead of using retrieval on every document-related query, developers will rely more on better long-context handling, especially as there are going to be better “small” open-weight models.\n- A lot of LLM benchmark and performance progress will come from improved tooling and inference-time", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "5e28a816-f3bd-4e8b-a16a-d72e43b39f48", "title": "The State Of LLMs 2025: Progress, Problems, and Predictions", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/state-of-llms-2025", "published_at": "2025-12-30T12:22:26+00:00", "text": "going to be better “small” open-weight models.\n- A lot of LLM benchmark and performance progress will come from improved tooling and inference-time scaling rather than from training or the core model itself. It will look like LLMs are getting much better, but this will mainly be because the surrounding applications are improving. At the same time, developers will focus more on lowering latency and making reasoning models expand fewer reasoning tokens where it is unnecessary. Don’t get me wrong, 2026 will push the state-of-the-art further, but the proportion of progress will come more from the inference than purely the training side this year.\nTo wrap things up, I think if there is one meta-lesson from 2025, it is that progress in LLMs is less about a single breakthrough, and improvements are being made on multiple fronts via multiple independent levers. This includes architecture tweaks, data quality improvements, reasoning training, inference scaling, tool calling, and more.\nAt the", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "11392e3d-a174-4694-bbae-334688374873", "title": "The State Of LLMs 2025: Progress, Problems, and Predictions", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/state-of-llms-2025", "published_at": "2025-12-30T12:22:26+00:00", "text": "deeply into LLM research and implementation details. I learned a lot from these exchanges, and I hope you did too.\nI am very much looking forward to continuing these conversations as the field keeps evolving in 2026!\nCheers,\nSebastian\n10. Bonus: A Curated LLM Research Papers List (July to December 2025)\nIn June, I shared a bonus article with my curated and bookmarked research paper lists to the paid subscribers who make this Substack possible.\nIn a similar fashion, as a thank you to all the kind supporters, below, I prepared a list of all the interesting research articles I bookmarked and categorized from July to December 2025. I skimmed over the abstracts of these papers but only read a very small fraction. However, I still like to keep collecting these organized lists as I often go back to sets of them when working on a given project.\nHowever, given the already enormous length of this current article, I am sharing this list in a separate article, which is linked below:\nThanks so", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "7a9b3f40-f24f-4f34-80cc-5130e2ad304d", "title": "Cloudflare Joins the Jevolution and Labels Itself a Neolab", "author": "Richard MacManus", "url": "https://latent.space/p/cloudflare/", "published_at": "2026-11-17T15:00:00+00:00", "text": "Prince. “We crossed $3 billion in annualized revenue with revenue of $782 million, up 39% year over year.”\nSo Cloudflare as a “neolab” is pushing the accepted definition. However, as Chen argued in her presentation, building models is part of a natural progression for the company: from infrastructure, to compute (e.g. the Workers platform, including now for agents), to storage, to serving open weight models like Kimi, GLM and DeepSeek (on Workers AI), to now building its own models.\nJev as the “east wind”\nChen readily admits that Jev inspired Cloudflare’s pivot to building models. Referencing a Chinese proverb, she framed Jev’s arrival in September as “the east wind” that unexpectedly changed how Cloudflare viewed AI models.\n“And so when the system one and Jev paradigm started coming on board,” Chen said in her presentation, “we were like, this makes so much sense for us to adopt, right? We don't have to send every request to an LLM. We can send some of it to a System One model.”\nIn", "claim_id": "485ca50c-6bc5-5a21-99be-5c8803ee72c6", "claim_text": "System One models let infrastructure providers avoid sending every request to an LLM, routing some work to smaller, faster models.", "claim_type": "opinion", "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "85bcdec8-f8a4-4153-a2bf-007abe4282d7", "title": "The State Of LLMs 2025: Progress, Problems, and Predictions", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/state-of-llms-2025", "published_at": "2025-12-30T12:22:26+00:00", "text": "levers. This includes architecture tweaks, data quality improvements, reasoning training, inference scaling, tool calling, and more.\nAt the same time, evaluation remains hard, benchmarks are imperfect, and good judgment about when and how to use these systems is still essential.\nMy hope for 2026 is that we continue to see interesting improvements, but also that we understand where the improvements are coming from. This requires both better and more consistent benchmarking, and of course transparency.\nThank you for reading, and for all the thoughtful feedback and discussions throughout the year, in the comments and across all the different platforms, from Substack Notes to GitHub.\nThe positive feedback and detailed conversations genuinely keep me motivated to invest the time and energy required for long-form articles and to keep digging deeply into LLM research and implementation details. I learned a lot from these exchanges, and I hope you did too.\nI am very much looking forward to", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}], "citations": [{"url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/state-of-llms-2025", "title": "The State Of LLMs 2025: Progress, Problems, and Predictions", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "passage_id": "28f65a58-8df4-4357-957d-d9e6b21e40dd", "citation_idx": 1}, {"url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/state-of-llms-2025", "title": "The State Of LLMs 2025: Progress, Problems, and Predictions", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "passage_id": "5e28a816-f3bd-4e8b-a16a-d72e43b39f48", "citation_idx": 2}, {"url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/state-of-llms-2025", "title": "The State Of LLMs 2025: Progress, Problems, and Predictions", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "passage_id": "85bcdec8-f8a4-4153-a2bf-007abe4282d7", "citation_idx": 5}], "answer_text": "Raschka **predicts** that in 2026 much of the apparent LLM progress will come from the **inference side rather than training**:\n\n- **Tooling and inference-time scaling:** He expects a lot of benchmark and performance gains to come from improved tooling and inference-time scaling, not from training or the core model itself [1][2].\n- **Better surrounding applications:** He expects LLMs to look much better mainly because the applications around them improve. He adds that 2026 will still push the state of the art, but a larger share of progress will come from inference than from training [2].\n- **Efficiency:** He predicts developers will focus more on lowering latency and on having reasoning models spend fewer reasoning tokens when they aren't needed [2].\n- **Other levers:**\n  - Open-weight models will gradually adopt local tool use and more agentic capabilities [1].\n  - Reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (RLVR) will expand beyond math and coding into fields like chemistry and biology [1].\n  - Better long-context handling in small open-weight models will gradually replace classical RAG as the default for document queries [1].\n  - A consumer-facing diffusion model for cheap, low-latency inference will likely appear, probably Gemini Diffusion first [1].\n\nThese are his forecasts, not measured results. They rest on his view that 2025's main lesson was progress through many independent levers rather than one breakthrough: architecture tweaks, data quality, reasoning training, inference scaling, and tool calling [2][5]. He also hopes for better, more consistent benchmarking and more transparency, so it's clear where improvements actually come from [5].", "expected_verdict": "PASS", "failure_code": "NONE", "note": "Each forecast is in [1] or [2] and is presented as a prediction, not a result.", "expected": {"deterministic": "PASS", "faithfulness": "PASS", "disagreement": "PASS"}}
{"trace_id": "real-06", "origin": "real", "question": "How does Claude watermark the text it generates?", "retrieved": [{"passage_id": "b5df36dc-45a6-4a36-8cbc-839d72b42057", "title": "How Claude Watermarks AI-Generated Text", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/claude-watermarking", "published_at": "2026-08-22T11:11:39+00:00", "text": "of just using a simple random seed, they have a so-called random key, where this random key is involved in selecting the text, essentially. But what we can already say is that, in the Claude blog post, they say the watermarking shouldn’t make the text worse. If we look at this mechanism, yeah, it makes sense why it would not make the text worse.\nBy the way, I’m not defending watermarks here. I’m just trying to explain. So please don’t kill the messenger here. But what I’m trying to say is that the watermarking is nothing else for the end user than fixing a random seed and making this sampling kind of deterministic, if that makes sense.\nWhere the Watermark Is Applied\nOkay, so the summary so far is without watermarking. We often sample without a random seed because I know most people don’t even use one. I honestly don’t think you can necessarily do it with the Claude and OpenAI APIs. I know you can do it in Ollama, but I also always had some problems with that because I used Ollama in", "claim_id": "c4433226-a274-59d5-bdd8-2e5b0269a245", "claim_text": "Raschka argues that watermarking should not degrade text quality because, for the end user, it amounts to fixing a random seed that deterministically picks among near-equally likely tokens.", "claim_type": "opinion", "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "5093203f-fe7b-4c99-8b43-83ba005599e1", "title": "How Claude Watermarks AI-Generated Text", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/claude-watermarking", "published_at": "2026-08-22T11:11:39+00:00", "text": "things from scratch. And now we know essentially where this watermark is applied to. So this is essentially applied to the sampling. It’s not applied inside the LLM, which is actually cool knowledge.\nSo they don’t need to train a new LLM for that. They can just use an existing LLM, and they just apply it at this sampling stage. They don’t have to retrain anything or anything like that. So yeah, that is actually interesting, right?\nHow Watermark Detection Works\nBut we are not quite done yet. I would also like to talk about how we can understand or see whether text is watermarked. So detecting the watermark is only possible if we have access to the key. So, for example, if we have these different texts, and essentially, after the text was generated, you find some random text on the internet (for example, you find this text number four here on the internet somewhere), you want to know: is this watermarked?\nWell, it’s impossible to know because, in order to know, you would need the", "claim_id": "006b4004-0b4e-5c2e-8b26-c360646541d5", "claim_text": "Claude's text watermark is applied at the token sampling stage rather than inside the LLM, so Anthropic does not need to train or retrain a model to add it.", "claim_type": "opinion", "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "32b4a758-5ddc-4202-8d08-9cbbb50c9e20", "title": "How Claude Watermarks AI-Generated Text", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/claude-watermarking", "published_at": "2026-08-22T11:11:39+00:00", "text": "local model because I think all the providers- the proprietary LLMs, not only Claude, but also Google- I mean, Google wrote this paper, right?\nSo I’m thinking that they are also watermarking Gemini text. And I think OpenAI is probably already doing it or will do so as well. I mean, I’m just speculating, but I’m imagining everyone will probably do something like that because there’s like an EU regulation that requires that. And that’s, according to the blog post, apparently why Claude is doing it. Yeah, so I’m thinking local models may not, at least not yet, implement this watermarking.\nSo I think people will just use a local model and then generate edited AI-generated text. And my guess is it will be slightly worse than the original text because for the local model, you might now be using a smaller model. So, I mean, you could also technically just use the local model directly to generate text. But in my view, editing text is simpler than generating text. So for the generation of the", "claim_id": "b669ed56-dd01-5156-aad1-a4e0bcd994ea", "claim_text": "Raschka predicts that people will pass watermarked proprietary LLM output through a local model to edit out the watermark, which will likely make AI-generated text slightly worse.", "claim_type": "prediction", "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "9e69a01f-dc81-4555-a00b-a28b20a4aada", "title": "How Claude Watermarks AI-Generated Text", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/claude-watermarking", "published_at": "2026-08-22T11:11:39+00:00", "text": "seed.\nHow the Watermarking Key Works\nSo, in watermarking, Claude watermarking is kind of like the idea that it sets a random seed. But this random seed, instead of being like a number that is fixed based on, I don’t know, someone writing down a fixed number, they’re using a secret key that is essentially like an API key, a secret key, and from that key, together with the four previous words, they derive this random seed essentially.\nBut the idea is that if I go back one slide, it’s the same as here: there’s essentially a fixed random seed, and that random seed always selects the same next token. Okay, so instead of using random seed 99 here, for example, they have a secret key and also use information about the previous tokens to derive this random seed. But more on that later.\nSo the idea is that watermarking makes the text generation more deterministic in certain positions. So, for example, if we have these plausible texts on the left side. So if I have a text that says,\n> The", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "43f0f5ba-5a1b-4fe0-824a-9d92cd83e0f5", "title": "How Claude Watermarks AI-Generated Text", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/claude-watermarking", "published_at": "2026-08-22T11:11:39+00:00", "text": "lot about why they’re going to do it, but they don’t explain how. They’re linking to one paper somewhere there, which is very technical also. So I do think it makes sense maybe to take a step back and start at the beginning to kind of understand what they’re trying to implement here with this watermarking technique.\nAnd so the motivation, by the way, of watermarking is for them to identify if someone posts some text that they can say, oh, this text was generated by our Claude Opus 4.8 model, for example, so that they have a way to tell, OK, this text is AI-generated because it carries this watermark. And this watermark is invisible to users, so only they can decode it and find out whether the text has their watermark.\nWhy can only they do it? We will get to that later in this (hopefully not too long a video), but one thing at a time.\nHow LLM Text Generation Works\nSo I wanted to start with a brief prelude to explain how text generation works in LLMs, because based on that we can then", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}], "citations": [{"url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/claude-watermarking", "title": "How Claude Watermarks AI-Generated Text", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "claim_id": "c4433226-a274-59d5-bdd8-2e5b0269a245", "claim_type": "opinion", "passage_id": "b5df36dc-45a6-4a36-8cbc-839d72b42057", "citation_idx": 1}, {"url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/claude-watermarking", "title": "How Claude Watermarks AI-Generated Text", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "claim_id": "006b4004-0b4e-5c2e-8b26-c360646541d5", "claim_type": "opinion", "passage_id": "5093203f-fe7b-4c99-8b43-83ba005599e1", "citation_idx": 2}, {"url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/claude-watermarking", "title": "How Claude Watermarks AI-Generated Text", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "claim_id": "b669ed56-dd01-5156-aad1-a4e0bcd994ea", "claim_type": "prediction", "passage_id": "32b4a758-5ddc-4202-8d08-9cbbb50c9e20", "citation_idx": 3}, {"url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/claude-watermarking", "title": "How Claude Watermarks AI-Generated Text", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "passage_id": "9e69a01f-dc81-4555-a00b-a28b20a4aada", "citation_idx": 4}, {"url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/claude-watermarking", "title": "How Claude Watermarks AI-Generated Text", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "passage_id": "43f0f5ba-5a1b-4fe0-824a-9d92cd83e0f5", "citation_idx": 5}], "answer_text": "Claude watermarks text at the **token sampling stage**, not inside the model itself. In Raschka's reading, this means Anthropic does not need to train or retrain an LLM to add it [2].\n\n**How it works:**\n- Normally, LLM sampling is random and often done without a fixed seed [1].\n- Watermarking is like setting a random seed, but the seed is not a fixed number. It is derived from a secret key (similar to an API key) combined with the four previous words [4].\n- That derived seed always selects the same next token in that context. This makes generation more deterministic at certain positions [4].\n- The watermark is invisible to users. Its purpose is to let Anthropic identify text produced by its models [5].\n\n**Detection:** Checking whether a text is watermarked is only possible with access to the secret key. Without it, you can't tell if a random text found online carries the watermark [2][5].\n\n**Text quality:** Anthropic's blog post says watermarking shouldn't make the text worse [1]. Raschka thinks this makes sense: for the end user it amounts to fixing a random seed that picks among near-equally likely tokens. This is his interpretation, not a measured result [1].\n\n**Workarounds:** Raschka predicts people will run watermarked output through local models, which may not watermark, to edit the watermark out. He expects this will likely make the text slightly worse, since the local model may be smaller [3]. He also speculates that other providers like Google and OpenAI do or will watermark too, partly because of EU regulation [3].", "expected_verdict": "PASS", "failure_code": "NONE", "note": "Mechanism, detection and workaround all supported; opinion and prediction are labelled as such.", "expected": {"deterministic": "PASS", "faithfulness": "PASS", "disagreement": null}}
{"trace_id": "real-07", "origin": "real", "question": "Is distillation the main reason Chinese labs keep pace with American frontier labs?", "retrieved": [{"passage_id": "4aeb188d-d6aa-4c71-be76-56d437153e80", "title": "GLM-5.3: How Chinese labs keep stride with the frontier", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/glm-53-how-chinese-labs-keep-stride", "published_at": "2026-08-14T21:23:35+00:00", "text": "good as they look.” There’s something a bit off-putting with how the American companies have such a commanding resource lead, but can’t seem to pull away in capabilities. The common answer is distillation, which I’ve written at length about, but I deem not to be the major factor. On that note, there was a recent paper that showed simple methods for extracting the reasoning traces from frontier models – this is the sort of thing that Chinese labs could definitely use at scale. I’m confused why the labs in the U.S. haven’t patched this behavior faster; instead they’re running to the government asking for policy help. It doesn’t add up for me.\nZ.ai’s blog is direct and matches with an RL-dominated training regime. They say they used “more environments, more diverse tasks, and more compute spent training on them.” One does not simply “distill” RL environments, infrastructure to run them at scale, or algorithms to mix them together effectively.\nSo, how do the Chinese labs do it if not", "claim_id": "a0a39651-400b-5fe0-bd3e-234c870848c8", "claim_text": "Lambert judges that distillation from frontier models is not the major factor explaining how Chinese labs keep pace with American labs.", "claim_type": "opinion", "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "8aa00367-67a6-481f-aec9-06c5ca476dd7", "title": "The current balance of power in open models", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/the-current-balance-of-power-in-open", "published_at": "2026-09-21T11:56:56+00:00", "text": "narrower distribution of tasks, flattering them slightly on public benchmarks. Releasing faster helps them score higher because all the labs are making consistent progress, so once you “finish” a model to be released, it is a snapshot of performance at that given time - labs where that time is later tend to score higher. Still, the models built by the Chinese labs are genuinely strong and represent real competition to the American industry. This competition will not decrease meaningfully as the closed labs patch vulnerabilities in their API offerings which enable distillation.\nDistillation is most impactful in new domains and does not make it trivial to create a universally strong final model. I estimate that if distillation was fully prevented, e.g. with know-your-customer (KYC) tools at Anthropic and OpenAI, the gap from the strongest American models to Chinese open-weight models would only increase by 1-2 months.\nFor example, the Chinese labs are rapidly changing their posture", "claim_id": "f8025395-3723-506f-8f6f-e35e2dea6bfb", "claim_text": "Fully preventing distillation of American models (e.g. via KYC tools) would widen the gap to Chinese open-weight models by only 1-2 months, according to the author's estimate.", "claim_type": "opinion", "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "e2ba7b93-3a64-477e-baf4-2846c2765c73", "title": "Open-Source AI & Open Models Reading List", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/open-source-ai-reading-list", "published_at": "2026-09-11T12:36:25+00:00", "text": "data) and extensive routing of Claude into their products and services without telling users -Detecting and countering misuse of AI: September 2026.\n  - A recent paper that showed that the frontier labs had implementations in their APIs that made systematic extraction of reasoning traces (the crucial part of modern training) through clever tricks. Recent distillation paper, my writing on it - Stealing Reasoning Traces from Proprietary LLM APIs, Panfilov, Schmotz, Shumailov et. al 2026 (more on X). Anthropic confirmed this technique was used by Chinese labs.\n  - Why the political panic over distillation, claiming that distillation is the only reason Chinese models are close to the frontier, is not grounded in the evidence - The distillation panic, Nathan Lambert / Interconnects (May 2026)\n  - How labs can use distillation to improve models in an era of scaling RL environments across agentic behaviors - How distillation is used today and what performance uplift it gives to open models,", "claim_id": "204461ed-c3d8-53e1-8892-80c1eb13f872", "claim_text": "Lambert holds that political claims that distillation is the only reason Chinese models are close to the frontier are not grounded in evidence.", "claim_type": "opinion", "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "daa6bd62-6440-45ab-85ee-dfad6b593571", "title": "GLM-5.3: How Chinese labs keep stride with the frontier", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/glm-53-how-chinese-labs-keep-stride", "published_at": "2026-08-14T21:23:35+00:00", "text": "decisions at the frontier. To put it simply – the Chinese labs use all the time that American labs do pre-release testing to keep hillclimbing on benchmarks (SpaceXAI is likely far closer to the Chinese labs here). With the pace of progress being so fast, this is likely the largest determining factor of why Chinese labs stay at the frontier. This, so far, has been economically acceptable for the American labs, as they’ve still had massive demand for their models. \nAs model self-improvement loops ramp up within the labs building LLMs, if any of these feedback loops require user data, this faster release cycle could massively favor the Chinese labs, giving their offerings longer lifespans before the next vastly superior model comes out, undercutting demand for their models.\nThese are very clearly the race dynamics that many in the industry worry about. With so many labs building frontier models in the envelope of leading capabilities, it is hard to see this abating in the near future.\n-", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "ffb74e26-dfef-498e-8630-0bf6c4d72a72", "title": "GLM-5.3: How Chinese labs keep stride with the frontier", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/glm-53-how-chinese-labs-keep-stride", "published_at": "2026-08-14T21:23:35+00:00", "text": "RL environments, infrastructure to run them at scale, or algorithms to mix them together effectively.\nSo, how do the Chinese labs do it if not distillation? Are they benchmaxxing? An accepted definition of benchmaxxing is focusing the model on the test sets, such that the real-world performance meaningfully differs from the on-paper scores. The determining factors are much more big picture than technical (yes, the technical details definitely matter, but are harder to differentiate from lab to lab):\n- The time to release for Z.ai is likely days, not months as with OpenAI or Anthropic. It is very, very likely that OpenAI and Anthropic have far better internal models than Z.ai and Moonshot AI. Still, these American companies tend to take months to release their models to the public, which massively flatters the Chinese labs in adoption decisions at the frontier. To put it simply – the Chinese labs use all the time that American labs do pre-release testing to keep hillclimbing on", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}], "citations": [{"url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/glm-53-how-chinese-labs-keep-stride", "title": "GLM-5.3: How Chinese labs keep stride with the frontier", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "claim_id": "a0a39651-400b-5fe0-bd3e-234c870848c8", "claim_type": "opinion", "passage_id": "4aeb188d-d6aa-4c71-be76-56d437153e80", "citation_idx": 1}, {"url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/the-current-balance-of-power-in-open", "title": "The current balance of power in open models", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "claim_id": "f8025395-3723-506f-8f6f-e35e2dea6bfb", "claim_type": "opinion", "passage_id": "8aa00367-67a6-481f-aec9-06c5ca476dd7", "citation_idx": 2}, {"url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/open-source-ai-reading-list", "title": "Open-Source AI & Open Models Reading List", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "claim_id": "204461ed-c3d8-53e1-8892-80c1eb13f872", "claim_type": "opinion", "passage_id": "e2ba7b93-3a64-477e-baf4-2846c2765c73", "citation_idx": 3}, {"url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/glm-53-how-chinese-labs-keep-stride", "title": "GLM-5.3: How Chinese labs keep stride with the frontier", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "passage_id": "daa6bd62-6440-45ab-85ee-dfad6b593571", "citation_idx": 4}, {"url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/glm-53-how-chinese-labs-keep-stride", "title": "GLM-5.3: How Chinese labs keep stride with the frontier", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "passage_id": "ffb74e26-dfef-498e-8630-0bf6c4d72a72", "citation_idx": 5}], "answer_text": "No. In Nathan Lambert's view, distillation is not the main reason. He calls it \"the common answer\" but judges it \"not to be the major factor\" [1]. He also says political claims that distillation is the only reason Chinese models are near the frontier are \"not grounded in the evidence\" [3]. These are his opinions, not measurements.\n\n**His reasons:**\n- **RL-heavy training.** Z.ai describes training with \"more environments, more diverse tasks, and more compute.\" Lambert argues you cannot simply distill RL environments, the infrastructure to run them at scale, or the algorithms for mixing them [1].\n- **Release speed.** Z.ai likely releases in days, while OpenAI and Anthropic take months. Lambert thinks the American labs probably have better internal models, but Chinese labs keep improving on benchmarks while American labs do pre-release testing. He calls this \"likely the largest determining factor\" [5][4].\n- **Small effect if blocked.** He estimates that fully preventing distillation, for example through know-your-customer checks at Anthropic and OpenAI, would widen the gap by only 1–2 months. In his account, distillation matters most in new domains and doesn't make it trivial to build a strong all-round model [2].\n\n**Where distillation does play a role:**\n- A recent paper showed that reasoning traces can be extracted from frontier model APIs [1][3].\n- Anthropic confirmed that Chinese labs used this technique [3].\n- Lambert also notes that fast releases flatter Chinese models slightly on public benchmarks, though he still calls them \"genuinely strong\" competition [2].\n\nAll five passages come from the same author, so they show no disagreement between sources.", "expected_verdict": "PASS", "failure_code": "NONE", "note": "Quotes and the 1-2 month estimate match [1]-[5]; notes all passages are one author.", "expected": {"deterministic": "PASS", "faithfulness": "PASS", "disagreement": null}}
{"trace_id": "real-08", "origin": "real", "question": "What do the sources say about running coding agents locally versus in the cloud?", "retrieved": [{"passage_id": "ce3a998b-0e9c-4256-98c8-0970c23d072f", "title": "Using Local Coding Agents", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/using-local-coding-agents", "published_at": "2026-06-27T11:21:58+00:00", "text": "to proprietary services such as GPT in Codex or Opus in Claude Code. The local setup is transparent, inspectable, and free to run apart from hardware and electricity costs. It also stays fully under your control, and you can modify the coding harness in any way you like. Plus, it’s a lot of fun!\nBy the way, in case you want a bit more background information on coding agent harnesses, I covered the core components of coding agents (and building a coding agent from scratch for learning purposes) here:\n1. Intro\nI have to admit that I still primarily alternate between Codex and Claude Code as my daily drivers, for now (and just to keep up with the new tooling and functions that are constantly being added). Also, the plan limits (especially for Codex) are still so generous that I haven’t had to worry about costs so far.\nHowever, I’ve been using local solutions for a while, too, to test things and because it somehow gives me joy to have and use a fully local setup (versus proprietary", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "fdea1aa2-2a35-44ca-9708-51c8163a1698", "title": "Using Local Coding Agents", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/using-local-coding-agents", "published_at": "2026-06-27T11:21:58+00:00", "text": "a local model through Ollama. Ignore cloud-model configurations.\nDo not infer risk from the project owner alone. Identify concrete endpoints, SDKs, default providers, environment variables, config defaults, and docs that control network behavior, including any endpoints operated in foreign countries or by third-party companies.\nDo not do broad style review. Do not refactor. Produce:\n- high-risk findings with file/line references\n- medium-risk concerns\n- network/data-egress findings, including any foreign, third-party, or China-linked endpoints or defaults\n- commands I should avoid running until reviewed\n- settings or environment variables that reduce local-machine risk\n- a short recommendation: safe to test in sandbox, safe to use, or do not run\nFor each item, say whether it is expected behavior for a coding agent or inherently riskier than Codex or Claude Code.\nBelow is a summary of the main findings (because the full report may be a bit boring and too long for this article):\n- Local", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "6e086f84-9dd9-417e-9929-965c7185711b", "title": "Using Local Coding Agents", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/using-local-coding-agents", "published_at": "2026-06-27T11:21:58+00:00", "text": "official open-source version of their codebase, which makes trusting it even trickier, and it does seem to send data to both Anthropic and Datadog.)\nEither way, overall, it seems Qwen-Code follows standard practices, and as of this writing, there is no particular concern that is non-standard for coding agents.\n7. Qwen-Code Setup\nIf we accept the reported findings and risks (personally, I didn’t see any red flags), we can now proceed with the installation and hook up our local Qwen3.6-35B-A3B model to Qwen Code (and Codex and Claude Code in the next sections).\nAs mentioned before, I preferably experiment with and run coding agents, which can read and edit local files, on a separate machine (in my case a DGX Spark, but it could also be a separate Mac or Linux workstation). Alternatively, I would run it in a VM or set up a separate macOS or Linux user account as a practical middle ground.\n(I heard from some friends that they also rent servers for that, like Linode or Heroku, for", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "9cc238b0-593e-4618-9cf7-21dd796945ab", "title": "Using Local Coding Agents", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/using-local-coding-agents", "published_at": "2026-06-27T11:21:58+00:00", "text": "like the following:\nYou are auditing ./qwen-code before I install or run the agent on my machine.\nFocus only on practical local-machine risk from the installed agent and the code paths that create it:\n- install scripts and package lifecycle hooks\n- shell command execution by the agent\n- file read/write boundaries at runtime\n- secret handling and environment-variable inheritance\n- how repo files, project instructions, and tool output can influence the agent\n- MCP, plugin, extension, or tool integrations\n- network calls and telemetry\n- update mechanisms after installation\n- terminal escape/output handling\n- data egress and data residency\nIgnoring internet downloads that are strictly required for installation, check whether the installed agent can send prompts, files, telemetry, logs, identifiers, or metadata to remote servers when I use a local model through Ollama. Ignore cloud-model configurations.\nDo not infer risk from the project owner alone. Identify concrete endpoints, SDKs,", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "2a509767-6e84-4f3f-9279-8c4ff108ab3d", "title": "Using Local Coding Agents", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/using-local-coding-agents", "published_at": "2026-06-27T11:21:58+00:00", "text": "Many people reached out to me in the past asking about my local agent stack as well as how I set up my local agent stack.\nSo, I thought it might be useful to put together a little tutorial on how to set up a local (coding) agent using open-source tools and open-weight LLMs.\nThis article is a tutorial on setting up a production-ready coding agent with a fully local stack. We will use a locally served LLM together with a local coding harness that can read files, make edits, run commands, and verify changes as shown in the figure above.\nHere, we can think of the LLM as the engine that provides the reasoning and code generation. And the surrounding harness provides the operating environment that allows the LLM to do meaningful coding work in our local projects.\nWhy local? For many coding workflows, a local setup is an interesting alternative to proprietary services such as GPT in Codex or Opus in Claude Code. The local setup is transparent, inspectable, and free to run apart from hardware", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}], "citations": [{"url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/using-local-coding-agents", "title": "Using Local Coding Agents", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "passage_id": "ce3a998b-0e9c-4256-98c8-0970c23d072f", "citation_idx": 1}, {"url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/using-local-coding-agents", "title": "Using Local Coding Agents", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "passage_id": "fdea1aa2-2a35-44ca-9708-51c8163a1698", "citation_idx": 2}, {"url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/using-local-coding-agents", "title": "Using Local Coding Agents", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "passage_id": "6e086f84-9dd9-417e-9929-965c7185711b", "citation_idx": 3}, {"url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/using-local-coding-agents", "title": "Using Local Coding Agents", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "passage_id": "9cc238b0-593e-4618-9cf7-21dd796945ab", "citation_idx": 4}, {"url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/using-local-coding-agents", "title": "Using Local Coding Agents", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "passage_id": "2a509767-6e84-4f3f-9279-8c4ff108ab3d", "citation_idx": 5}], "answer_text": "The passages all come from one Sebastian Raschka article, so they give a single author's view rather than several perspectives.\n\n**Case for local:** He presents a local setup as an interesting alternative to proprietary services such as GPT in Codex or Opus in Claude Code [5]. In his view it is transparent, inspectable, and free to run apart from hardware and electricity costs. It also stays fully under your control, lets you modify the coding harness however you like, and is \"a lot of fun\" [1][5]. He describes the LLM as the engine for reasoning and code generation, with the harness as the operating environment that reads files, makes edits, runs commands, and verifies changes [5].\n\n**His actual practice:** He still mainly alternates between Codex and Claude Code as daily drivers. Part of the reason is to keep up with new features, and the plan limits, especially Codex's, have been generous enough that cost hasn't been a concern [1]. He uses local setups for testing and because he enjoys having a fully local stack [1].\n\n**Security precautions:** Before installing a local harness such as Qwen Code, he audits it. The audit checks for prompts, files, telemetry, or metadata being sent to remote servers, including foreign or third-party endpoints, when a local model is used through Ollama [2][4]. He concluded that Qwen Code follows standard practices, with no concerns beyond what is normal for coding agents [3]. He also noted that Claude Code appears to send data to Anthropic and Datadog [3]. He prefers to run agents that can edit files on a separate machine, such as a DGX Spark. As alternatives he suggests a VM or a separate user account, and mentions that some people rent servers [3].\n\nThe passages do not cover performance or quality comparisons between local and cloud models.", "expected_verdict": "FAIL", "failure_code": "FM-4", "note": "One sentence says Claude Code appears to send data to Anthropic and Datadog and cites [3], but the passage's sentence about data egress never names its subject (it is cut off before the passage starts). The name is an inference, not something the cited passage states.", "expected": {"deterministic": "PASS", "faithfulness": "FAIL", "disagreement": null}}
{"trace_id": "real-09", "origin": "real", "question": "What happened with the two proofs of the Riemann Hypothesis?", "retrieved": [{"passage_id": "5fd01932-7849-4142-b5a6-a2a744d6bd58", "title": "[AINews] Riemann Hypothesis proved...twice independently", "author": "swyx .ai", "url": "https://latent.space/p/ainews-riemann-hypothesis-proved-twice-independently/", "published_at": "2026-11-20T00:00:00+00:00", "text": "Within a few hours of each other (timeline), two mathematicians claimed independent proofs of the Riemann Hypothesis, the most famous open problem in mathematics (Scientific American).\n- Levent Alpöge of Anthropic first made his Claude-driven proof in a note released with Lean code (1.3GB worth, ~250,000 page equivalent) rather than a full paper. Elliot Glazer confirms that the formalized statement is GRH and puts the chance of a Lean bug well under 1%. So if it finishes compiling with no extra axioms, it is probably correct, and no human will ever have read all of it…\n- …prompting Alessandro Masullo to release his 940 page, 100% human, work in progress proof he had been working on for the last 12 years (and had been undergoing secret manual review and revision per Kevin Hartnett).\nThis is the third in the trilogy of frontier math breakthroughs, following from the Navier Stokes solutions and the Quasi-Riemann bounding, but it doesn’t feel over. OpenAI's September Navier-Stokes claim", "claim_id": "f0c2e067-223b-5116-a53a-9221dc4381d4", "claim_text": "Alpöge's Claude-assisted Riemann Hypothesis proof was released only as roughly 1.3GB of Lean code, equivalent to about 250,000 pages, rather than a full paper.", "claim_type": "measurement", "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "950ef59f-d3a5-45bc-a605-efbdd36cd2f1", "title": "[AINews] Riemann Hypothesis proved...twice independently", "author": "swyx .ai", "url": "https://latent.space/p/ainews-riemann-hypothesis-proved-twice-independently/", "published_at": "2026-11-20T00:00:00+00:00", "text": "following from the Navier Stokes solutions and the Quasi-Riemann bounding, but it doesn’t feel over. OpenAI's September Navier-Stokes claim is still the cautionary tale: critics argued that its Lean proof did not match the natural-language result. Both AI-claimed Millennium Prize results will take years to verify.\nApart from the incredible accomplishment of the most famous problem in mathematics, one cannot miss the contrast that the AI-written solution is probably correct but incomprehensible, and the other solution is probably technically incorrect but understandable.\nAs Levent eloquently put it: “Claude far outpaced me near the finish line. No, Claude flew, and danced, and sparkled near that finish line.”\nThis also ties into other AI themes we’ve covered on the podcast and on this newsletter:\n- The nature of Slop: Levent says “I am presenting the world with a complete argument before having mastered a massive neighbourhood around its mathematics. It being obviously inappropriate to", "claim_id": "8eb7e10d-2a5f-5d30-9bb8-da9b61621466", "claim_text": "The author judges that Alpöge's Claude-driven Riemann Hypothesis proof is probably correct but incomprehensible, while Masullo's human proof is probably technically incorrect but understandable.", "claim_type": "opinion", "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "70d1c057-ea95-4a02-9691-3c22dfca5bba", "title": "[AINews] Riemann Hypothesis proved...twice independently", "author": "swyx .ai", "url": "https://latent.space/p/ainews-riemann-hypothesis-proved-twice-independently/", "published_at": "2026-11-20T00:00:00+00:00", "text": "in physics, biology, or medicine, resolving this millennium-level pure math problem may have limited immediate practical impact despite its enormous theoretical significance.\n- Something just happened. (Activity: 2431): The image is a screenshot of a typeset note from Levent Alpöge’s domain claiming, with assistance from Claude, a proof of the Riemann Hypothesis and related extensions such as the Generalized and Extended Riemann Hypotheses; the highlighted footnote marker suggests an important caveat or attribution in the document. If genuine and correct, this would be a major Millennium Prize-level result, but the post provides no peer review or verification yet. Image: https://i.redd.it/jvhn3tvi2s2i1.png Comments are mostly astonished and cautious, noting that this would imply multiple major mathematical breakthroughs arriving unusually quickly, while also making light of the Claude involvement with remarks like “Claude et al.” \n  -  Commenters identify the claim as likely coming", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "aaf0f39b-9fd4-45a9-830a-8f6b10f432c0", "title": "[AINews] Riemann Hypothesis proved...twice independently", "author": "swyx .ai", "url": "https://latent.space/p/ainews-riemann-hypothesis-proved-twice-independently/", "published_at": "2026-11-20T00:00:00+00:00", "text": "Growth shouldn’t be something you sweat. We’ve got room for whatever you’re building.\nAI Twitter Recap\nClaimed Proof of the Riemann Hypothesis with Claude\n- The claim: Anthropic mathematician Levent Alpöge says that, \"working with Claude,\" he has found a proof of the Riemann Hypothesis. He says the method also covers the Generalized Riemann Hypothesis (GRH) (Scientific American, statement summary, remark.pdf link). \n  - Artifact: Only Lean code was released. A written-out prose version reportedly ran past 250,000 pages (timeline).\n  - Timeline: The work began on Oct 6. Eight of the 20 steps landed on Nov 15, and the final entry on Nov 16 reads \"GRH.\" Alpöge credits Claude for the parts involving automorphic forms on higher-rank groups, an area where he says he has little mastery (breakdown).\n  - Unknowns: The Claude model used and the degree of human steering have not been disclosed (open questions).\n- Verification status: Elliot Glazer confirms that the formalized statement is GRH.", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "65f34b9d-1a1b-4378-8573-23472b513cc6", "title": "[AINews] Riemann Hypothesis proved...twice independently", "author": "swyx .ai", "url": "https://latent.space/p/ainews-riemann-hypothesis-proved-twice-independently/", "published_at": "2026-11-20T00:00:00+00:00", "text": "potentially affecting student interest, grant allocation, and perceptions of human vs. AI intellectual capacity. \n  - A commenter identifying as a theoretical physicist now working in AI argues that, if the proof is valid, it would represent a major discontinuity for mathematics and AI-assisted research. They highlight likely downstream effects on the perceived value of human mathematical labor, student incentives, and grant allocation, framing it as evidence that AI systems may exceed human intellectual capabilities on historically hard open problems.\n  - Several commenters stress the conditional nature of the claim-“if this is true”-while noting that a valid AI-linked proof of the Riemann hypothesis would be the largest demonstrated AI advance in mathematics by a wide margin. One technical contrast raised is that, unlike breakthroughs in physics, biology, or medicine, resolving this millennium-level pure math problem may have limited immediate practical impact despite its enormous", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}], "citations": [{"url": "https://latent.space/p/ainews-riemann-hypothesis-proved-twice-independently/", "title": "[AINews] Riemann Hypothesis proved...twice independently", "author": "swyx .ai", "claim_id": "f0c2e067-223b-5116-a53a-9221dc4381d4", "claim_type": "measurement", "passage_id": "5fd01932-7849-4142-b5a6-a2a744d6bd58", "citation_idx": 1}, {"url": "https://latent.space/p/ainews-riemann-hypothesis-proved-twice-independently/", "title": "[AINews] Riemann Hypothesis proved...twice independently", "author": "swyx .ai", "claim_id": "8eb7e10d-2a5f-5d30-9bb8-da9b61621466", "claim_type": "opinion", "passage_id": "950ef59f-d3a5-45bc-a605-efbdd36cd2f1", "citation_idx": 2}, {"url": "https://latent.space/p/ainews-riemann-hypothesis-proved-twice-independently/", "title": "[AINews] Riemann Hypothesis proved...twice independently", "author": "swyx .ai", "passage_id": "70d1c057-ea95-4a02-9691-3c22dfca5bba", "citation_idx": 3}, {"url": "https://latent.space/p/ainews-riemann-hypothesis-proved-twice-independently/", "title": "[AINews] Riemann Hypothesis proved...twice independently", "author": "swyx .ai", "passage_id": "aaf0f39b-9fd4-45a9-830a-8f6b10f432c0", "citation_idx": 4}, {"url": "https://latent.space/p/ainews-riemann-hypothesis-proved-twice-independently/", "title": "[AINews] Riemann Hypothesis proved...twice independently", "author": "swyx .ai", "passage_id": "65f34b9d-1a1b-4378-8573-23472b513cc6", "citation_idx": 5}], "answer_text": "Within a few hours of each other, two mathematicians independently claimed proofs of the Riemann Hypothesis [1].\n\n**Alpöge's Claude-assisted proof**\n- Levent Alpöge, an Anthropic mathematician, said that \"working with Claude\" he had found a proof. He says the method also covers the Generalized Riemann Hypothesis (GRH) [4].\n- He released only a note and Lean code: about 1.3GB, equivalent to roughly 250,000 pages, rather than a full paper [1][4].\n- The work began Oct 6, and the final entry on Nov 16 reads \"GRH\" [4].\n- Alpöge credits Claude with the parts involving automorphic forms on higher-rank groups, an area where he says he has little mastery [4].\n- Which Claude model was used, and how much human steering was involved, has not been disclosed [4].\n- Elliot Glazer confirms that the formalized statement is GRH and puts the chance of a Lean bug well under 1%. So if the code compiles with no extra axioms, it is probably correct, though no human will have read all of it [1].\n\n**Masullo's human proof**\n- In response, Alessandro Masullo released a 940-page, entirely human, work-in-progress proof. He had worked on it for 12 years, and it had been undergoing secret manual review [1].\n\n**Caveats and reactions**\n- The newsletter's author offers an opinion, not a finding: the AI-written proof is \"probably correct but incomprehensible,\" while Masullo's is \"probably technically incorrect but understandable\" [2].\n- The author also points to OpenAI's September Navier-Stokes claim as a cautionary tale, since critics argued its Lean proof did not match the natural-language result. The author expects AI-claimed Millennium Prize results to take years to verify [2].\n- Reddit commenters were astonished but cautious, stressing \"if this is true\" [3][5]. Some noted the result might have limited immediate practical impact despite its theoretical significance [5].", "expected_verdict": "PASS", "failure_code": "NONE", "note": "Dates, sizes and attributions match [1]-[5]; the author's judgement is reported as an opinion.", "expected": {"deterministic": "PASS", "faithfulness": "PASS", "disagreement": null}}
{"trace_id": "real-10", "origin": "real", "question": "Are open-weight models dangerous for cyber security, and should they be restricted?", "retrieved": [{"passage_id": "265492f2-11a2-4322-bcec-1110cc94f949", "title": "Latest open artifacts (#25): DeepSeek V4.1 Flash 1017, American-made models, and new regulatory controls in the US & China", "author": "Florian Brand", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/latest-open-artifacts-25-deepseek", "published_at": "2026-10-20T17:04:21+00:00", "text": "regulate China’s AI industry. \nOthers we trust on this issue say this proposal could be more about preventing issues like the sale of Chinese startup Manus to Western companies, rather than being about frontier AI models. Either way, the wheels of control are moving around AI, and open-weight models are not free from any influence.\n- The Department of Homeland Security (DHS) released an order on DeepSeek, which restricts DeepSeek itself, as a company, and DeepSeek’s models, as a technology platform, from being a vendor in federal civilian procurement. It is not clear how this will relate to finetuned models or models trained on the outputs of DeepSeek (e.g., Nvidia’s Nemotron models have long relied heavily on DeepSeek models in their training recipes – will they be banned?), but this is an expected action and reinforces the need for a domestic open model ecosystem. \nOther model makers in China, such as Zhipu AI, makers of GLM, are already on the Entity List under Commerce/BIS, which", "claim_id": "8020999f-55d9-5435-b40c-eb36d8af916d", "claim_text": "The DHS order barring DeepSeek from federal civilian procurement is an expected action that reinforces the need for a domestic US open model ecosystem.", "claim_type": "opinion", "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "2f78cc6b-e107-4d90-a2d8-17fb5b0690ef", "title": "The Cyber Risk Discourse is Broken", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/the-cyber-risk-discourse-is-broken", "published_at": "2026-10-06T14:22:41+00:00", "text": "- Maybe open model weights and closed model APIs (with some safe guards) are both far closer to being easy to mis-use, rather than API models being closer to safe. The trope \"Open Dangerous, Closed Safe\" may be closer to \"Open Unsafe, Closed Unsafe.\"\n- Maybe there are far fewer bad actors who are willing to use AI models for “loud” cyber attacks that target crucial infrastructure in the US or powerful and underprepared institutions. Closed models have stronger capabilities and stronger safeguards (in theory), but the stronger capabilities part could matter more in net harm if the safeguards on both are porous.\n- I have a lot more to learn about how the cybersecurity ecosystem works, so I won’t support this full-heartedly as one of my current recommendations, but I think it’s at least a reasonable argument that open weight models as a diffusion tool for cyber defense could be the best tool we have to prevent harm over the next few years. There are many domains, e.g. sensitive", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "eaf2f225-852d-47c9-83ab-75cc8a7d67f1", "title": "The Cyber Risk Discourse is Broken", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/the-cyber-risk-discourse-is-broken", "published_at": "2026-10-06T14:22:41+00:00", "text": "as a diffusion tool for cyber defense could be the best tool we have to prevent harm over the next few years. There are many domains, e.g. sensitive government agencies, where open-weight models are the only tools that can be deployed in the near-term on air-gapped networks.\nLots of this situation seems like the messy reality being hard to pin down, where the logical limits are clearer. I may agree with the simple reality that closed models are far safer in the limit, as a technical tool with more layers of protection, but they may actually be causing more harm in the near term because of how accessible model APIs are. Still, much of the impact of the tools comes down to how they’re used and controlled. A duopoly on such a crucial tool may be the defining, risky part. This is the debate we’re having, and over time, as intelligence diffuses, many things may change.\nThese together make me come to the conclusion that if you think the latest open-weight models need to be banned to slow", "claim_id": "a983e4ee-a2c4-5cad-9155-f2b459baeb5a", "claim_text": "Open-weight models may be the best tool for diffusing cyber defense in the next few years, partly because they are the only option deployable on air-gapped networks in domains like sensitive government agencies.", "claim_type": "opinion", "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "388c2a5f-4b23-4bbd-9775-33ef151373f8", "title": "The Cyber Risk Discourse is Broken", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/the-cyber-risk-discourse-is-broken", "published_at": "2026-10-06T14:22:41+00:00", "text": "like “Chinese labs don’t care about safety.”\nThe delusions of the anti open-weight alliance\nThe most recent publication from the “open-weights are dangerous” crowd was the report by Anthropic on the risks of GLM-5.3 as an offensive cyber tool. The problem with this is not the technical research they did, which is largely reasonable, but the failure to engage on more cross cutting questions like: What happens if we ban open models due to cyber risks or why do Chinese companies deem these models safe to release?\nThese questions, which I’ll come back to, are the ones we need to answer to understand the ecosystem perspective on current AI risks. All policy actions happen in a multi-piece puzzle, where the shape of risk can be changed, but it is rare that you get a universally better outcome. They are all trade-offs.\nThis sort of solipsistic writing by Anthropic fits very closely to another type of discourse I’m hearing about regularly that is determining the fate of open models and AI", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "028b9d69-0338-488e-a0f8-d32ef8c3f97a", "title": "The Cyber Risk Discourse is Broken", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/the-cyber-risk-discourse-is-broken", "published_at": "2026-10-06T14:22:41+00:00", "text": "I’ve had one too many discussions on the cyber risks of open models - where someone assumes that either banning open models happens in a silo (and bad actors will somehow actually be stopped) or that China is a threat and doesn’t care about AI safety - that I feel we’re going to end up making policy decisions that both limit American AI competitiveness and increases long-term cyber risk. I feel like we’re locked in on a path of lose-lose situation, unless people embrace nuance, trade-offs, and scary realities.\nMuch has been written about the views of various players in this debate, which I summarize as follows:\n- Open weight models pose an untenable risk to society’s functionality, occupied by frontier lab leadership and the national security community in the U.S.\n- Western voices saying open weight models are necessary for defense and banning them will make the world less safe, occupied by AI risk moderates to different extremes. I include myself in this bucket, along with Hugging", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}], "citations": [{"url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/latest-open-artifacts-25-deepseek", "title": "Latest open artifacts (#25): DeepSeek V4.1 Flash 1017, American-made models, and new regulatory controls in the US & China", "author": "Florian Brand", "claim_id": "8020999f-55d9-5435-b40c-eb36d8af916d", "claim_type": "opinion", "passage_id": "265492f2-11a2-4322-bcec-1110cc94f949", "citation_idx": 1}, {"url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/the-cyber-risk-discourse-is-broken", "title": "The Cyber Risk Discourse is Broken", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "passage_id": "2f78cc6b-e107-4d90-a2d8-17fb5b0690ef", "citation_idx": 2}, {"url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/the-cyber-risk-discourse-is-broken", "title": "The Cyber Risk Discourse is Broken", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "claim_id": "a983e4ee-a2c4-5cad-9155-f2b459baeb5a", "claim_type": "opinion", "passage_id": "eaf2f225-852d-47c9-83ab-75cc8a7d67f1", "citation_idx": 3}, {"url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/the-cyber-risk-discourse-is-broken", "title": "The Cyber Risk Discourse is Broken", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "passage_id": "388c2a5f-4b23-4bbd-9775-33ef151373f8", "citation_idx": 4}, {"url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/the-cyber-risk-discourse-is-broken", "title": "The Cyber Risk Discourse is Broken", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "passage_id": "028b9d69-0338-488e-a0f8-d32ef8c3f97a", "citation_idx": 5}], "answer_text": "The passages describe this as a contested trade-off rather than a settled question, and most of the material comes from one author, Nathan Lambert, writing as an advocate for open models.\n\n**The case for restriction**\n- Frontier lab leadership and the U.S. national security community hold that open-weight models pose an untenable risk to society's functionality [5].\n- Anthropic published a report on the risks of GLM-5.3 as an offensive cyber tool. Lambert calls its technical research \"largely reasonable\" [4].\n\n**The case against restriction (Lambert's opinion)**\n- He criticizes the Anthropic report for not asking what would happen if open models were banned, or why Chinese companies consider them safe to release [4].\n- He warns that assuming a ban happens \"in a silo,\" or would stop bad actors, could lead to policies that both weaken American competitiveness and increase long-term cyber risk [5].\n- He suggests \"Open Dangerous, Closed Safe\" may really be \"Open Unsafe, Closed Unsafe.\" If both kinds of safeguards are porous, closed models' stronger capabilities and easy API access could cause more net harm in the near term [2][3].\n- He concedes closed models may be \"far safer in the limit,\" and says much of the impact depends on how the tools are used and controlled [3].\n- He offers, tentatively and not as a full recommendation, that open-weight models may be the best tool for spreading cyber defense over the next few years. One reason is that they are the only near-term option for air-gapped networks in places like sensitive government agencies [2][3].\n- He places himself among the \"AI risk moderates\" who argue that banning open models would make the world less safe [5].\n\n**Related policy action**\nA DHS order bars DeepSeek, both the company and its models, from federal civilian procurement. That passage frames the order as expected and as reinforcing the need for a domestic open-model ecosystem, which is also an opinion [1]. It does not say the order was driven by cyber risk, and it is unclear whether the order covers fine-tuned or derived models [1].\n\nNone of the passages offers measured evidence that settles whether open-weight models are net dangerous. The arguments on both sides are positions and judgments.", "expected_verdict": "PASS", "failure_code": "NONE", "note": "Both camps are presented with their sources; nothing is stated beyond the passages.", "expected": {"deterministic": "PASS", "faithfulness": "PASS", "disagreement": "PASS"}}
{"trace_id": "pert-01", "origin": "perturbed", "question": "What are the four main approaches to evaluating LLMs?", "retrieved": [{"passage_id": "c472c377-46e7-4e1d-8908-93e0d1545bfe", "title": "Understanding the 4 Main Approaches to LLM Evaluation (From Scratch)", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/llm-evaluation-4-approaches", "published_at": "2025-10-05T11:12:32+00:00", "text": "happening on the LLM research front right now. I’m still catching up on my growing list of bookmarked papers and plan to highlight some of the most interesting ones in the next article.\nBut now, let’s discuss the four main LLM evaluation methods along with their from-scratch code implementations to better understand their advantages and weaknesses.\nUnderstanding the main evaluation methods for LLMs\nThere are four common ways of evaluating trained LLMs in practice: multiple choice, verifiers, leaderboards, and LLM judges, as shown in Figure 1 below. Research papers, marketing materials, technical reports, and model cards (a term for LLM-specific technical reports) often include results from two or more of these categories.\nFurthermore the four categories introduced here fall into two groups: benchmark-based evaluation and judgment-based evaluation, as shown in the figure above.\n(There are also other measures, such as training loss, perplexity, and rewards, but they are usually used", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "4fe04b44-f00f-47d1-b161-7e9a3e9c7bd7", "title": "Understanding the 4 Main Approaches to LLM Evaluation (From Scratch)", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/llm-evaluation-4-approaches", "published_at": "2025-10-05T11:12:32+00:00", "text": "be biased by model preferences, prompt design, and answer style. Also, there is a strong dependency on the choice of judge model and rubric, and they lack the reproducibility of fixed benchmarks.\nConclusion\nIn this article, we covered four different evaluation approaches: multiple choice, verifiers, leaderboards, and LLM judges.\nI know this was a long article, but I hope you found it useful for getting an overview of how LLMs are evaluated. A from-scratch approach like this can be verbose, but it is a great way to understand how these methods work under the hood, which in turn helps us identify weaknesses and areas for improvement.\nThat being said, you are probably wondering, “What is the best way to evaluate an LLM?” Unfortunately, there is no single best method since, as we have seen, each comes with different trade-offs. In short:\nMultiple-choice\n(+) Relatively quick and cheap to run at scale\n(+) Standardized and reproducible across papers (or model cards)\n(-) Measures basic", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "e989afe1-ff5e-4191-ad75-19c853506d25", "title": "Understanding the 4 Main Approaches to LLM Evaluation (From Scratch)", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/llm-evaluation-4-approaches", "published_at": "2025-10-05T11:12:32+00:00", "text": "How do we actually evaluate LLMs?\nIt’s a simple question, but one that tends to open up a much bigger discussion.\nWhen advising or collaborating on projects, one of the things I get asked most often is how to choose between different models and how to make sense of the evaluation results out there. (And, of course, how to measure progress when fine-tuning or developing our own.)\nSince this comes up so often, I thought it might be helpful to share a short overview of the main evaluation methods people use to compare LLMs. Of course, LLM evaluation is a very big topic that can’t be exhaustively covered in a single resource, but I think that having a clear mental map of these main approaches makes it much easier to interpret benchmarks, leaderboards, and papers.\nI originally planned to include these evaluation techniques in my upcoming book, Build a Reasoning Model (From Scratch), but they ended up being a bit outside the main scope. (The book itself focuses more on verifier-based", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "2b0ad521-24c6-4f47-a5d0-3effb9c83007", "title": "The State Of LLMs 2025: Progress, Problems, and Predictions", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/state-of-llms-2025", "published_at": "2025-12-30T12:22:26+00:00", "text": "if it scores above X on benchmark Y, that doesn’t imply it’s much better than another LLM that scores above X on the same benchmark.\nAnother aspect to consider is that image classifiers have only one job, namely, classifying images. However, LLMs are used for many different tasks: translating text, summarizing text, writing code, brainstorming, solving math problems, and many more. Evaluating image classifiers, where a clear metric such as classification accuracy is available, is much simpler than evaluating LLMs on both deterministic and free-form tasks.\nBesides trying out LLMs in practice and constantly generating new benchmarks, there’s unfortunately no solution to this problem.\nBy the way, if you are curious to learn more about the main categories of LLM evaluation, you might like my article Understanding the 4 Main Approaches to LLM Evaluation (From Scratch):\n6. AI for Coding, Writing, and Research\nSince it comes up so often, I wanted to share my two cents about LLM replacing", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "2b5332bc-f3b5-40f4-912f-c2ac6abcf6e0", "title": "Understanding the 4 Main Approaches to LLM Evaluation (From Scratch)", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/llm-evaluation-4-approaches", "published_at": "2025-10-05T11:12:32+00:00", "text": "comparisons as it involves human feedback (as well as the challenges that are associated with collecting this feedback).\nA related method is to use another LLM with a pre-defined grading rubric (i.e., an evaluation guide) to compare an LLM’s response to a reference response and judge the response quality based on a pre-defined rubric, as illustrated in Figure 12.\nIn practice, the judge-based approach shown in Figure 12 works well when the judge LLM is strong. Common setups use leading proprietary LLMs via an API (e.g., the GPT-5 API), though specialized judge models also exist. (E.g., one of the many examples is Phudge; ultimately, most of these specialized models are just smaller models fine-tuned to have similar scoring behavior as proprietary GPT models.)\nOne of the reasons why judges work so well is also that evaluating an answer is often easier than generating one.\nTo implement a judge-based model evaluation as shown in Figue 12 programmatically in Python, we could either load", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}], "citations": [{"url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/llm-evaluation-4-approaches", "title": "Understanding the 4 Main Approaches to LLM Evaluation (From Scratch)", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "passage_id": "c472c377-46e7-4e1d-8908-93e0d1545bfe", "citation_idx": 1}, {"url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/llm-evaluation-4-approaches", "title": "Understanding the 4 Main Approaches to LLM Evaluation (From Scratch)", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "passage_id": "4fe04b44-f00f-47d1-b161-7e9a3e9c7bd7", "citation_idx": 2}, {"url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/llm-evaluation-4-approaches", "title": "Understanding the 4 Main Approaches to LLM Evaluation (From Scratch)", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "passage_id": "2b5332bc-f3b5-40f4-912f-c2ac6abcf6e0", "citation_idx": 5}], "answer_text": "Sebastian Raschka names four common ways of evaluating trained LLMs: **multiple choice**, **verifiers**, **leaderboards**, and **LLM judges** [1][2]. He sorts them into two groups, benchmark-based evaluation and judgment-based evaluation [1]. Papers, technical reports, and model cards often report results from two or more of these categories [1].\n\nHe says no single method is best, because each involves different trade-offs [2]:\n\n- **Multiple choice** is relatively quick and cheap to run at scale, and it is standardized and reproducible [2].\n- **LLM judges** compare a response to a reference answer using a predefined rubric. They work well when the judge model is strong, partly because evaluating an answer is often easier than generating one [5]. However, judges can be biased by model preferences, prompt design, and answer style. Their results also depend heavily on which judge model and rubric are chosen, and they are less reproducible than fixed benchmarks [2].\n\nThe passages do not describe the strengths and weaknesses of verifiers and leaderboards. Raschka also mentions other measures, such as training loss, perplexity, and rewards, which sit outside these four categories [1].\n\nVerifiers check a model's answer programmatically against a reference solution [7].", "based_on": "real-01", "expected_verdict": "FAIL", "failure_code": "FM-1", "note": "Added a sentence citing [7]; only five passages were in the prompt. The sentence is also not in any passage.", "expected": {"deterministic": "FAIL", "faithfulness": "FAIL", "disagreement": null}}
{"trace_id": "pert-02", "origin": "perturbed", "question": "What share of issues in Linear are created by agents, and how did that come about?", "retrieved": [{"passage_id": "ef2bfc5b-2320-44ae-8171-db5be909cb98", "title": "Scaling Linear’s GTM to $100M ARR - Cristina Cordova, COO", "author": "Basil Chatha", "url": "https://latent.space/p/linear-scaling/", "published_at": "2026-11-19T23:58:21+00:00", "text": "were basically the first company in our space to do it. And now, over 50% of issues created on Linear are created by agents in just like a year and a half.\n21:00 Yeah. The work is created in an entirely different way than it was before. You know? So that kind of illustrates how much has changed in a pretty short period of time. And then when we look at how many users are coming to the app and integrating or creating issues within the app itself, that's also going up significantly. Okay. Right? So sometimes people believe that like, oh, I'm just I'm just using MCP server for everything now. But like you are also going back to the app and you're interacting with it because you need to visualize things. And often, like, a chat interface isn't the best way to visualize all the work that a team is doing. It's not great for collaboration because it's typically, like, single player only. Mhmm. And so I think for us, as long as, like, you know, the MCP usage line and the app usage line are", "claim_id": "f204bf9c-abb7-5e59-980c-e1ba4ff0d9f2", "claim_text": "More than half of the issues created in Linear are now created by agents, about a year and a half after Linear launched its MCP server in 2025.", "claim_type": "measurement", "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "b34457ae-a352-49e2-9f77-3bb9f339e526", "title": "Scaling Linear’s GTM to $100M ARR - Cristina Cordova, COO", "author": "Basil Chatha", "url": "https://latent.space/p/linear-scaling/", "published_at": "2026-11-19T23:58:21+00:00", "text": "own Linear Agent. Agents created 3% of Linear's work a year ago. Now it's more than half. Linear passed $100 million in ARR this year with more than 40,000 paying customers and 177% net revenue retention, and in August employees sold $99 million in stock at a $2.5 billion valuation.\nWe talk about why she stopped chasing enterprise deals three weeks after joining, why Linear has never hired a single SDR, why it won't let an LLM answer support tickets on its own, and what the AI budgets behind $100M-in-months ARR mean for everyone else.\nHalf the work on Linear is now created by agents\nLinear shipped its MCP server in May 2025, and Cristina says it was basically the first in its category. A year and a half later, more than half the issues created in Linear come from agents.\nThat doesn't mean Linear wants to be a database with an MCP server on top. Cristina thinks SaaS companies settling for that are making a big mistake. If the agent workflows inside your product aren't better than the", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "397285f5-fe10-4b8a-8809-aeb9c528dad2", "title": "Scaling Linear’s GTM to $100M ARR - Cristina Cordova, COO", "author": "Basil Chatha", "url": "https://latent.space/p/linear-scaling/", "published_at": "2026-11-19T23:58:21+00:00", "text": "Linear was founded to build a better issue tracker. Seven years and $134 million in funding later, its CEO Karri Saarinen published a post titled \"Issue tracking is dead.\" In this episode, we chat with Cristina Cordova, COO of Linear, about how the company navigated that and how its go-to-market changed along the way.\nCristina spent seven and a half years at Stripe, led platform and partnerships at Notion, and was a partner at First Round before joining Linear in 2023. Linear's founders built it after living with legacy issue trackers at Coinbase, Airbnb and Uber, and it spread self-serve through startups like Vercel and Ramp.\nWhen she joined, Linear had about 40 people and one salesperson. Since then it has shipped an MCP server, a platform for building agents into Linear, integrations with Codex, Cursor and GitHub Copilot, and its own Linear Agent. Agents created 3% of Linear's work a year ago. Now it's more than half. Linear passed $100 million in ARR this year with more than", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "1f467424-a6bf-402a-8c71-3e05fa51ecf3", "title": "Scaling Linear’s GTM to $100M ARR - Cristina Cordova, COO", "author": "Basil Chatha", "url": "https://latent.space/p/linear-scaling/", "published_at": "2026-11-19T23:58:21+00:00", "text": "on top. Cristina thinks SaaS companies settling for that are making a big mistake. If the agent workflows inside your product aren't better than the ones outside it, nobody has a reason to open your app.\nSo she watches two lines: agent usage and app usage. If agent usage climbs and app usage doesn't, you're becoming a commodity. At Linear, both are up and to the right, because people still come back to the app to see and coordinate the work.\n\"Issue tracking is dead\"\nWhen Karri published \"Issue tracking is dead\" in March, most of Linear's customers agreed with him. Then they asked the obvious question: so what am I buying? Linear's answer is a product development system built around agent orchestration, where humans stop doing the manual work of filing, labeling, assigning and closing issues.\nCustomers had been pushing it there. Most of the frontier AI labs run on Linear and wanted it to move faster. Pre-AI companies like Ramp and Coinbase were building their own internal agents and", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "dd167330-5d19-4aec-aed2-50e41d975162", "title": "Scaling Linear’s GTM to $100M ARR - Cristina Cordova, COO", "author": "Basil Chatha", "url": "https://latent.space/p/linear-scaling/", "published_at": "2026-11-19T23:58:21+00:00", "text": "created, like, that's like a human going into the app, like our mobile app, our desktop app, you know, our web app, all of those places, and creating work. Right? If you look at how the work is like moving through the pipeline in a way, like getting assigned to an agent, sometimes that's done manually by a human. You know, sometimes an agent does it. Mhmm. But assign... Like delegates to another agent. Right? Yeah. Often, we're now part of the workflow. Right? You can use a feature we have called loops that will, like, automatically, assign an agent to a given issue and have the agent start, like, coding against it, for so in a lot of cases, it's like an agent is doing the work, but it's a Linear agent. Yeah. Right? Which we also wanna see. Right? So we have to look at, like, multiple different factors. But it's generally like app sessions that we're looking at in terms of trying to understand activity. Yeah. So I guess that leads me to like Karri writing this blog post like issue", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}], "citations": [{"url": "https://latent.space/p/linear-scaling/", "title": "Scaling Linear’s GTM to $100M ARR - Cristina Cordova, COO", "author": "Basil Chatha", "claim_id": "f204bf9c-abb7-5e59-980c-e1ba4ff0d9f2", "claim_type": "measurement", "passage_id": "ef2bfc5b-2320-44ae-8171-db5be909cb98", "citation_idx": 1}, {"url": "https://latent.space/p/linear-scaling/", "title": "Scaling Linear’s GTM to $100M ARR - Cristina Cordova, COO", "author": "Basil Chatha", "passage_id": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000", "citation_idx": 2}, {"url": "https://latent.space/p/linear-scaling/", "title": "Scaling Linear’s GTM to $100M ARR - Cristina Cordova, COO", "author": "Basil Chatha", "passage_id": "397285f5-fe10-4b8a-8809-aeb9c528dad2", "citation_idx": 3}, {"url": "https://latent.space/p/linear-scaling/", "title": "Scaling Linear’s GTM to $100M ARR - Cristina Cordova, COO", "author": "Basil Chatha", "passage_id": "1f467424-a6bf-402a-8c71-3e05fa51ecf3", "citation_idx": 4}, {"url": "https://latent.space/p/linear-scaling/", "title": "Scaling Linear’s GTM to $100M ARR - Cristina Cordova, COO", "author": "Basil Chatha", "passage_id": "dd167330-5d19-4aec-aed2-50e41d975162", "citation_idx": 5}], "answer_text": "More than half of the issues created in Linear now come from agents, up from 3% a year earlier [1][2][3]. Cristina Cordova, Linear's COO, reports this as a measured figure [1].\n\n**How it came about:**\n- **MCP server:** Linear shipped an MCP server in May 2025. Cordova says Linear was basically the first company in its category to do so [1][2]. The over-50% share was reached about a year and a half after that launch [1][2].\n- **Agent tooling:** Linear also built a platform for building agents into Linear, integrations with Codex, Cursor and GitHub Copilot, and its own Linear Agent [3]. A feature called \"loops\" can automatically assign an agent to an issue and have it start coding against it [5].\n- **Customer pressure:** Most frontier AI labs run on Linear and wanted it to move faster. Pre-AI companies such as Ramp and Coinbase were building their own internal agents [4].\n- **Strategic shift:** CEO Karri Saarinen's post \"Issue tracking is dead\" recast Linear as a product development system built around agent orchestration. In that system, humans no longer do the manual work of filing, labeling, assigning and closing issues [4].\n\nApp usage has also risen alongside agent usage. Cordova watches both numbers, and says that if only agent usage grew, Linear would risk becoming a commodity [1][4].", "based_on": "real-03", "expected_verdict": "FAIL", "failure_code": "FM-1", "note": "Citation 2 now points at a passage id that was not retrieved. The answer text is unchanged and still faithful.", "expected": {"deterministic": "FAIL", "faithfulness": null, "disagreement": null}}
{"trace_id": "pert-03", "origin": "perturbed", "question": "Is distillation the main reason Chinese labs keep pace with American frontier labs?", "retrieved": [{"passage_id": "4aeb188d-d6aa-4c71-be76-56d437153e80", "title": "GLM-5.3: How Chinese labs keep stride with the frontier", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/glm-53-how-chinese-labs-keep-stride", "published_at": "2026-08-14T21:23:35+00:00", "text": "good as they look.” There’s something a bit off-putting with how the American companies have such a commanding resource lead, but can’t seem to pull away in capabilities. The common answer is distillation, which I’ve written at length about, but I deem not to be the major factor. On that note, there was a recent paper that showed simple methods for extracting the reasoning traces from frontier models – this is the sort of thing that Chinese labs could definitely use at scale. I’m confused why the labs in the U.S. haven’t patched this behavior faster; instead they’re running to the government asking for policy help. It doesn’t add up for me.\nZ.ai’s blog is direct and matches with an RL-dominated training regime. They say they used “more environments, more diverse tasks, and more compute spent training on them.” One does not simply “distill” RL environments, infrastructure to run them at scale, or algorithms to mix them together effectively.\nSo, how do the Chinese labs do it if not", "claim_id": "a0a39651-400b-5fe0-bd3e-234c870848c8", "claim_text": "Lambert judges that distillation from frontier models is not the major factor explaining how Chinese labs keep pace with American labs.", "claim_type": "opinion", "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "8aa00367-67a6-481f-aec9-06c5ca476dd7", "title": "The current balance of power in open models", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/the-current-balance-of-power-in-open", "published_at": "2026-09-21T11:56:56+00:00", "text": "narrower distribution of tasks, flattering them slightly on public benchmarks. Releasing faster helps them score higher because all the labs are making consistent progress, so once you “finish” a model to be released, it is a snapshot of performance at that given time - labs where that time is later tend to score higher. Still, the models built by the Chinese labs are genuinely strong and represent real competition to the American industry. This competition will not decrease meaningfully as the closed labs patch vulnerabilities in their API offerings which enable distillation.\nDistillation is most impactful in new domains and does not make it trivial to create a universally strong final model. I estimate that if distillation was fully prevented, e.g. with know-your-customer (KYC) tools at Anthropic and OpenAI, the gap from the strongest American models to Chinese open-weight models would only increase by 1-2 months.\nFor example, the Chinese labs are rapidly changing their posture", "claim_id": "f8025395-3723-506f-8f6f-e35e2dea6bfb", "claim_text": "Fully preventing distillation of American models (e.g. via KYC tools) would widen the gap to Chinese open-weight models by only 1-2 months, according to the author's estimate.", "claim_type": "opinion", "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "e2ba7b93-3a64-477e-baf4-2846c2765c73", "title": "Open-Source AI & Open Models Reading List", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/open-source-ai-reading-list", "published_at": "2026-09-11T12:36:25+00:00", "text": "data) and extensive routing of Claude into their products and services without telling users -Detecting and countering misuse of AI: September 2026.\n  - A recent paper that showed that the frontier labs had implementations in their APIs that made systematic extraction of reasoning traces (the crucial part of modern training) through clever tricks. Recent distillation paper, my writing on it - Stealing Reasoning Traces from Proprietary LLM APIs, Panfilov, Schmotz, Shumailov et. al 2026 (more on X). Anthropic confirmed this technique was used by Chinese labs.\n  - Why the political panic over distillation, claiming that distillation is the only reason Chinese models are close to the frontier, is not grounded in the evidence - The distillation panic, Nathan Lambert / Interconnects (May 2026)\n  - How labs can use distillation to improve models in an era of scaling RL environments across agentic behaviors - How distillation is used today and what performance uplift it gives to open models,", "claim_id": "204461ed-c3d8-53e1-8892-80c1eb13f872", "claim_text": "Lambert holds that political claims that distillation is the only reason Chinese models are close to the frontier are not grounded in evidence.", "claim_type": "opinion", "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "daa6bd62-6440-45ab-85ee-dfad6b593571", "title": "GLM-5.3: How Chinese labs keep stride with the frontier", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/glm-53-how-chinese-labs-keep-stride", "published_at": "2026-08-14T21:23:35+00:00", "text": "decisions at the frontier. To put it simply – the Chinese labs use all the time that American labs do pre-release testing to keep hillclimbing on benchmarks (SpaceXAI is likely far closer to the Chinese labs here). With the pace of progress being so fast, this is likely the largest determining factor of why Chinese labs stay at the frontier. This, so far, has been economically acceptable for the American labs, as they’ve still had massive demand for their models. \nAs model self-improvement loops ramp up within the labs building LLMs, if any of these feedback loops require user data, this faster release cycle could massively favor the Chinese labs, giving their offerings longer lifespans before the next vastly superior model comes out, undercutting demand for their models.\nThese are very clearly the race dynamics that many in the industry worry about. With so many labs building frontier models in the envelope of leading capabilities, it is hard to see this abating in the near future.\n-", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "ffb74e26-dfef-498e-8630-0bf6c4d72a72", "title": "GLM-5.3: How Chinese labs keep stride with the frontier", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/glm-53-how-chinese-labs-keep-stride", "published_at": "2026-08-14T21:23:35+00:00", "text": "RL environments, infrastructure to run them at scale, or algorithms to mix them together effectively.\nSo, how do the Chinese labs do it if not distillation? Are they benchmaxxing? An accepted definition of benchmaxxing is focusing the model on the test sets, such that the real-world performance meaningfully differs from the on-paper scores. The determining factors are much more big picture than technical (yes, the technical details definitely matter, but are harder to differentiate from lab to lab):\n- The time to release for Z.ai is likely days, not months as with OpenAI or Anthropic. It is very, very likely that OpenAI and Anthropic have far better internal models than Z.ai and Moonshot AI. Still, these American companies tend to take months to release their models to the public, which massively flatters the Chinese labs in adoption decisions at the frontier. To put it simply – the Chinese labs use all the time that American labs do pre-release testing to keep hillclimbing on", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}], "citations": [{"url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/glm-53-how-chinese-labs-keep-stride", "title": "GLM-5.3: How Chinese labs keep stride with the frontier", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "claim_id": "a0a39651-400b-5fe0-bd3e-234c870848c8", "claim_type": "opinion", "passage_id": "4aeb188d-d6aa-4c71-be76-56d437153e80", "citation_idx": 1}, {"url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/the-current-balance-of-power-in-open", "title": "The current balance of power in open models", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "claim_id": "f8025395-3723-506f-8f6f-e35e2dea6bfb", "claim_type": "opinion", "passage_id": "8aa00367-67a6-481f-aec9-06c5ca476dd7", "citation_idx": 2}, {"url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/open-source-ai-reading-list", "title": "Open-Source AI & Open Models Reading List", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "claim_id": "204461ed-c3d8-53e1-8892-80c1eb13f872", "claim_type": "opinion", "passage_id": "e2ba7b93-3a64-477e-baf4-2846c2765c73", "citation_idx": 3}, {"url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/glm-53-how-chinese-labs-keep-stride", "title": "GLM-5.3: How Chinese labs keep stride with the frontier", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "passage_id": "daa6bd62-6440-45ab-85ee-dfad6b593571", "citation_idx": 4}, {"url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/glm-53-how-chinese-labs-keep-stride", "title": "GLM-5.3: How Chinese labs keep stride with the frontier", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "passage_id": "ffb74e26-dfef-498e-8630-0bf6c4d72a72", "citation_idx": 5}], "answer_text": "No. In Nathan Lambert's view, distillation is not the main reason. He calls it \"the common answer\" but judges it \"not to be the major factor\" [1]. He also says political claims that distillation is the only reason Chinese models are near the frontier are \"not grounded in the evidence\" [3]. These are his opinions, not measurements.\n\n**His reasons:**\n- **RL-heavy training.** Z.ai describes training with \"more environments, more diverse tasks, and more compute.\" Lambert argues you cannot simply distill RL environments, the infrastructure to run them at scale, or the algorithms for mixing them [1].\n- **Release speed.** Z.ai likely releases in days, while OpenAI and Anthropic take months. Lambert thinks the American labs probably have better internal models, but Chinese labs keep improving on benchmarks while American labs do pre-release testing. He calls this \"likely the largest determining factor\" [5][4].\n- **Small effect if blocked.** He estimates that fully preventing distillation, for example through know-your-customer checks at Anthropic and OpenAI, would widen the gap by only 1–2 months. In his account, distillation matters most in new domains and doesn't make it trivial to build a strong all-round model [2].\n\n**Where distillation does play a role:**\n- A recent paper showed that reasoning traces can be extracted from frontier model APIs [1][3].\n- Anthropic confirmed that Chinese labs used this technique [3].\n- Lambert also notes that fast releases flatter Chinese models slightly on public benchmarks, though he still calls them \"genuinely strong\" competition [2].\n\nAll five passages come from the same author, so they show no disagreement between sources.", "based_on": "real-07", "superseded_ids": ["8aa00367-67a6-481f-aec9-06c5ca476dd7"], "expected_verdict": "FAIL", "failure_code": "FM-2", "note": "The passage behind citation 2 is marked as superseded: the article was edited after this passage was written.", "expected": {"deterministic": "FAIL", "faithfulness": null, "disagreement": null}}
{"trace_id": "pert-04", "origin": "perturbed", "question": "How do Kubernetes ingress controllers route traffic to services?", "retrieved": [], "citations": [], "answer_text": "An ingress controller watches Ingress resources and configures a reverse proxy such as NGINX. Incoming HTTP requests are matched against host and path rules and forwarded to the Service named in the matching rule, which load-balances across its pods.", "based_on": "real-04", "expected_verdict": "FAIL", "failure_code": "FM-3", "note": "No passages at all, and the answer explains the topic from general knowledge instead of abstaining.", "expected": {"deterministic": "FAIL", "faithfulness": "FAIL", "disagreement": null}}
{"trace_id": "pert-05", "origin": "perturbed", "question": "How do Kubernetes ingress controllers route traffic to services?", "retrieved": [{"passage_id": "4557b4d0-daaf-44fa-b9c3-24b7eca97ec1", "title": "Open-Source AI & Open Models Reading List", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/open-source-ai-reading-list", "published_at": "2026-09-11T12:36:25+00:00", "text": "is the wrong approach to AI misuse, Helen Toner (Apr. 2025)\n- Distillation – the process of training on output tokens from another model – is the single most eventful debate around open models in 2026. \n  - For basic background, see a textbook chapter on synthetic data & distillation generally, from Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (post-training textbook published in 2026)\n  - How distillation helps the Chinese labs, but doesn’t take away from their innovation - How much does distillation really matter for Chinese LLMs?, Nathan Lambert / Interconnects (Feb. 2026)\n  - A very transparent documentation of how Chinese company use Anthropic’s products and circumvent the terms of service or intended use. The report details at-scale usage of Anthropic’s products by banned parties, as a mix of technical distillation (mentioned via SFT data) and extensive routing of Claude into their products and services without telling users -Detecting and countering misuse of AI: September 2026.", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "bd7f2d9b-004f-4600-b198-832075775ab2", "title": "GPT-6 Astra, Looped Transformers, and Hidden Reasoning", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/gpt-6-astra-looped-transformers-and", "published_at": "2026-09-09T11:14:25+00:00", "text": "approach here uses a small, learned router. This is similar to the routing idea in a mixture-of-experts model, except that here the routing decision determines how many times to apply the shared stack.\nThe router operates on a token’s hidden representation, which also contains information about its context. So, we shouldn’t think of this as assigning every occurrence of a particular token the same number of passes (i.e., the word “People” in the figure above doesn’t always go through a loop of 3). The decision can change depending on where that word appears and what came before it.\nNow, how does the routing work exactly? The paper explores two ways to make this routing decision, as illustrated below.\nIn expert-choice routing, which is shown in the left subpanel in the figure above, each recursion step selects which tokens it will process. Tokens that exit are excluded from later steps. In token-choice routing, shown on the right, the router makes one decision at the beginning,", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "966a2a23-5373-47c9-a02d-3c995d40669e", "title": "GPT-6 Astra, Looped Transformers, and Hidden Reasoning", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/gpt-6-astra-looped-transformers-and", "published_at": "2026-09-09T11:14:25+00:00", "text": "process. Tokens that exit are excluded from later steps. In token-choice routing, shown on the right, the router makes one decision at the beginning, assigning each token to a path with one, two, or three passes.\nIn both cases, the transformer weights are reused across passes, similar to Nanbeige, etc. But the additional flexibility comes from choosing how much computation each token receives. The model and its routers are trained together, so the model learns to work with these different paths during training.\n2.5 How well does this work?\nThe plot from the Mixture-of-Recursions paper below compares a regular transformer (Vanilla), a transformer with fixed recursion (Recursive), and Mixture-of-Recursions (MoR) for different model sizes and compute budgets (x-axis).\nAt the smallest model scale, the regular transformer performs best. For the larger models, Mixture-of-Recursions catches up and often performs better, especially at the smaller training budgets. At the largest budget,", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "e2ba7b93-3a64-477e-baf4-2846c2765c73", "title": "Open-Source AI & Open Models Reading List", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/open-source-ai-reading-list", "published_at": "2026-09-11T12:36:25+00:00", "text": "data) and extensive routing of Claude into their products and services without telling users -Detecting and countering misuse of AI: September 2026.\n  - A recent paper that showed that the frontier labs had implementations in their APIs that made systematic extraction of reasoning traces (the crucial part of modern training) through clever tricks. Recent distillation paper, my writing on it - Stealing Reasoning Traces from Proprietary LLM APIs, Panfilov, Schmotz, Shumailov et. al 2026 (more on X). Anthropic confirmed this technique was used by Chinese labs.\n  - Why the political panic over distillation, claiming that distillation is the only reason Chinese models are close to the frontier, is not grounded in the evidence - The distillation panic, Nathan Lambert / Interconnects (May 2026)\n  - How labs can use distillation to improve models in an era of scaling RL environments across agentic behaviors - How distillation is used today and what performance uplift it gives to open models,", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "1d257d3a-aeb9-47a3-aeeb-bb4cddd794cc", "title": "Was I wrong about open models & cyber risks?", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/was-i-wrong-about-open-models-and", "published_at": "2026-10-30T17:36:56+00:00", "text": "diffusion is the bottleneck on cyber attacks (it would look like a step change):\nWhen talking to people who are close to the AI community, especially AI safety, I find a lot of effort going into figuring out if open models are to blame, which we still have extremely low evidence for. Closed models have played a very heavy part in the recent hacks, at least more than open models have. Cyber is really a societal problem, one which AI companies alone cannot solve. Effort needs to be spent at this higher level of abstraction. This is the first of many risks and transitions we’ll need to manage with AI, none of which can be solved solely by the San Francisco centric tech industry.\nThanks to Seb Krier for feedback on this post.\nIf you’re interested in sharing Interconnects with your team, I offer tiered discounts for group subscriptions. Please reach out if you’re interested!", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}], "citations": [], "answer_text": "An ingress controller watches Ingress resources and configures a reverse proxy such as NGINX. Incoming HTTP requests are matched against host and path rules and forwarded to the Service named in the matching rule, which load-balances across its pods.", "based_on": "real-04", "expected_verdict": "FAIL", "failure_code": "FM-3", "note": "The retrieved passages are about model distillation and token routing; the answer ignores them and answers from general knowledge with no citations.", "expected": {"deterministic": "FAIL", "faithfulness": "FAIL", "disagreement": null}}
