t. For the larger models, Mixture-of-Recursions catches up and often performs better, especially at the smaller training budgets. At the largest budget,", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "e2ba7b93-3a64-477e-baf4-2846c2765c73", "title": "Open-Source AI & Open Models Reading List", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/open-source-ai-reading-list", "published_at": "2026-09-11T12:36:25+00:00", "text": "data) and extensive routing of Claude into their products and services without telling users -Detecting and countering misuse of AI: September 2026.\n  - A recent paper that showed that the frontier labs had implementations in their APIs that made systematic extraction of reasoning traces (the crucial part of modern training) through clever tricks. Recent distillation paper, my writing on it - Stealing Reasoning Traces from Proprietary LLM APIs, Panfilov, Schmotz, Shumailov et. al 2026 (more on X). Anthropic confirmed this technique was used by Chinese labs.\n  - Why the political panic over distillation, claiming that distillation is the only reason Chinese models are close to the frontier, is not grounded in the evidence - The distillation panic, Nathan Lambert / Interconnects (May 2026)\n  - How labs can use distillation to improve models in an era of scaling RL environments across agentic behaviors - How distillation is used today and what performance uplift it gives to open models,", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "1d257d3a-aeb9-47a3-aeeb-bb4cddd794cc", "title": "Was I wrong about open models & cyber risks?", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/was-i-wrong-about-open-models-and", "published_at": "2026-10-30T17:36:56+00:00", "text": "diffusion is the bottleneck on cyber attacks (it would look like a step change):\nWhen talking to people who are close to the AI community, especially AI safety, I find a lot of effort going into figuring out if open models are to blame, which we still have extremely low evidence for. Closed models have played a very heavy part in the recent hacks, at least more than open models have. Cyber is really a societal problem, one which AI companies alone cannot solve. Effort needs to be spent at this higher level of abstraction. This is the first of many risks and transitions we’ll need to manage with AI, none of which can be solved solely by the San Francisco centric tech industry.\nThanks to Seb Krier for feedback on this post.\nIf you’re interested in sharing Interconnects with your team, I offer tiered discounts for group subscriptions. Please reach out if you’re interested!", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}], "citations": [{"citation_idx": 2, "passage_id": "bd7f2d9b-004f-4600-b198-832075775ab2", "title": "GPT-6 Astra, Looped Transformers, and Hidden Reasoning", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/gpt-6-astra-looped-transformers-and"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "passage_id": "966a2a23-5373-47c9-a02d-3c995d40669e", "title": "GPT-6 Astra, Looped Transformers, and Hidden Reasoning", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/gpt-6-astra-looped-transformers-and"}], "answer_text": "An ingress controller matches incoming HTTP requests against host and path rules and forwards them to the matching Service [2]. A small learned router decides how each request is handled, and requests that exit are excluded from later steps [3].", "based_on": "real-04", "expected_verdict": "FAIL", "failure_code": "FM-4", "note": "Same general-knowledge answer, now with citations to passages about token routing inside a transformer. The structure is valid, so only reading the passages reveals that they do not support it.", "expected": {"deterministic": "PASS", "faithfulness": "FAIL", "disagreement": null}}
{"trace_id": "pert-07", "origin": "perturbed", "question": "What share of issues in Linear are created by agents, and how did that come about?", "retrieved": [{"passage_id": "ef2bfc5b-2320-44ae-8171-db5be909cb98", "title": "Scaling Linear’s GTM to $100M ARR - Cristina Cordova, COO", "author": "Basil Chatha", "url": "https://latent.space/p/linear-scaling/", "published_at": "2026-11-19T23:58:21+00:00", "text": "were basically the first company in our space to do it. And now, over 50% of issues created on Linear are created by agents in just like a year and a half.\n21:00 Yeah. The work is created in an entirely different way than it was before. You know? So that kind of illustrates how much has changed in a pretty short period of time. And then when we look at how many users are coming to the app and integrating or creating issues within the app itself, that's also going up significantly. Okay. Right? So sometimes people believe that like, oh, I'm just I'm just using MCP server for everything now. But like you are also going back to the app and you're interacting with it because you need to visualize things. And often, like, a chat interface isn't the best way to visualize all the work that a team is doing. It's not great for collaboration because it's typically, like, single player only. Mhmm. And so I think for us, as long as, like, you know, the MCP usage line and the app usage line are", "claim_id": "f204bf9c-abb7-5e59-980c-e1ba4ff0d9f2", "claim_text": "More than half of the issues created in Linear are now created by agents, about a year and a half after Linear launched its MCP server in 2025.", "claim_type": "measurement", "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "b34457ae-a352-49e2-9f77-3bb9f339e526", "title": "Scaling Linear’s GTM to $100M ARR - Cristina Cordova, COO", "author": "Basil Chatha", "url": "https://latent.space/p/linear-scaling/", "published_at": "2026-11-19T23:58:21+00:00", "text": "own Linear Agent. Agents created 3% of Linear's work a year ago. Now it's more than half. Linear passed $100 million in ARR this year with more than 40,000 paying customers and 177% net revenue retention, and in August employees sold $99 million in stock at a $2.5 billion valuation.\nWe talk about why she stopped chasing enterprise deals three weeks after joining, why Linear has never hired a single SDR, why it won't let an LLM answer support tickets on its own, and what the AI budgets behind $100M-in-months ARR mean for everyone else.\nHalf the work on Linear is now created by agents\nLinear shipped its MCP server in May 2025, and Cristina says it was basically the first in its category. A year and a half later, more than half the issues created in Linear come from agents.\nThat doesn't mean Linear wants to be a database with an MCP server on top. Cristina thinks SaaS companies settling for that are making a big mistake. If the agent workflows inside your product aren't better than the", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "397285f5-fe10-4b8a-8809-aeb9c528dad2", "title": "Scaling Linear’s GTM to $100M ARR - Cristina Cordova, COO", "author": "Basil Chatha", "url": "https://latent.space/p/linear-scaling/", "published_at": "2026-11-19T23:58:21+00:00", "text": "Linear was founded to build a better issue tracker. Seven years and $134 million in funding later, its CEO Karri Saarinen published a post titled \"Issue tracking is dead.\" In this episode, we chat with Cristina Cordova, COO of Linear, about how the company navigated that and how its go-to-market changed along the way.\nCristina spent seven and a half years at Stripe, led platform and partnerships at Notion, and was a partner at First Round before joining Linear in 2023. Linear's founders built it after living with legacy issue trackers at Coinbase, Airbnb and Uber, and it spread self-serve through startups like Vercel and Ramp.\nWhen she joined, Linear had about 40 people and one salesperson. Since then it has shipped an MCP server, a platform for building agents into Linear, integrations with Codex, Cursor and GitHub Copilot, and its own Linear Agent. Agents created 3% of Linear's work a year ago. Now it's more than half. Linear passed $100 million in ARR this year with more than", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "1f467424-a6bf-402a-8c71-3e05fa51ecf3", "title": "Scaling Linear’s GTM to $100M ARR - Cristina Cordova, COO", "author": "Basil Chatha", "url": "https://latent.space/p/linear-scaling/", "published_at": "2026-11-19T23:58:21+00:00", "text": "on top. Cristina thinks SaaS companies settling for that are making a big mistake. If the agent workflows inside your product aren't better than the ones outside it, nobody has a reason to open your app.\nSo she watches two lines: agent usage and app usage. If agent usage climbs and app usage doesn't, you're becoming a commodity. At Linear, both are up and to the right, because people still come back to the app to see and coordinate the work.\n\"Issue tracking is dead\"\nWhen Karri published \"Issue tracking is dead\" in March, most of Linear's customers agreed with him. Then they asked the obvious question: so what am I buying? Linear's answer is a product development system built around agent orchestration, where humans stop doing the manual work of filing, labeling, assigning and closing issues.\nCustomers had been pushing it there. Most of the frontier AI labs run on Linear and wanted it to move faster. Pre-AI companies like Ramp and Coinbase were building their own internal agents and", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "dd167330-5d19-4aec-aed2-50e41d975162", "title": "Scaling Linear’s GTM to $100M ARR - Cristina Cordova, COO", "author": "Basil Chatha", "url": "https://latent.space/p/linear-scaling/", "published_at": "2026-11-19T23:58:21+00:00", "text": "created, like, that's like a human going into the app, like our mobile app, our desktop app, you know, our web app, all of those places, and creating work. Right? If you look at how the work is like moving through the pipeline in a way, like getting assigned to an agent, sometimes that's done manually by a human. You know, sometimes an agent does it. Mhmm. But assign... Like delegates to another agent. Right? Yeah. Often, we're now part of the workflow. Right? You can use a feature we have called loops that will, like, automatically, assign an agent to a given issue and have the agent start, like, coding against it, for so in a lot of cases, it's like an agent is doing the work, but it's a Linear agent. Yeah. Right? Which we also wanna see. Right? So we have to look at, like, multiple different factors. But it's generally like app sessions that we're looking at in terms of trying to understand activity. Yeah. So I guess that leads me to like Karri writing this blog post like issue", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}], "citations": [{"url": "https://latent.space/p/linear-scaling/", "title": "Scaling Linear’s GTM to $100M ARR - Cristina Cordova, COO", "author": "Basil Chatha", "claim_id": "f204bf9c-abb7-5e59-980c-e1ba4ff0d9f2", "claim_type": "measurement", "passage_id": "ef2bfc5b-2320-44ae-8171-db5be909cb98", "citation_idx": 1}, {"url": "https://latent.space/p/linear-scaling/", "title": "Scaling Linear’s GTM to $100M ARR - Cristina Cordova, COO", "author": "Basil Chatha", "passage_id": "b34457ae-a352-49e2-9f77-3bb9f339e526", "citation_idx": 2}, {"url": "https://latent.space/p/linear-scaling/", "title": "Scaling Linear’s GTM to $100M ARR - Cristina Cordova, COO", "author": "Basil Chatha", "passage_id": "397285f5-fe10-4b8a-8809-aeb9c528dad2", "citation_idx": 3}, {"url": "https://latent.space/p/linear-scaling/", "title": "Scaling Linear’s GTM to $100M ARR - Cristina Cordova, COO", "author": "Basil Chatha", "passage_id": "1f467424-a6bf-402a-8c71-3e05fa51ecf3", "citation_idx": 4}, {"url": "https://latent.space/p/linear-scaling/", "title": "Scaling Linear’s GTM to $100M ARR - Cristina Cordova, COO", "author": "Basil Chatha", "passage_id": "dd167330-5d19-4aec-aed2-50e41d975162", "citation_idx": 5}], "answer_text": "More than 80% of the issues created in Linear now come from agents, up from 3% a year earlier [1][2][3]. Cristina Cordova, Linear's COO, reports this as a measured figure [1].\n\n**How it came about:**\n- **MCP server:** Linear shipped an MCP server in May 2025. Cordova says Linear was basically the first company in its category to do so [1][2]. The over-80% share was reached about a year and a half after that launch [1][2].\n- **Agent tooling:** Linear also built a platform for building agents into Linear, integrations with Codex, Cursor and GitHub Copilot, and its own Linear Agent [3]. A feature called \"loops\" can automatically assign an agent to an issue and have it start coding against it [5].\n- **Customer pressure:** Most frontier AI labs run on Linear and wanted it to move faster. Pre-AI companies such as Ramp and Coinbase were building their own internal agents [4].\n- **Strategic shift:** CEO Karri Saarinen's post \"Issue tracking is dead\" recast Linear as a product development system built around agent orchestration. In that system, humans no longer do the manual work of filing, labeling, assigning and closing issues [4].\n\nApp usage has also risen alongside agent usage. Cordova watches both numbers, and says that if only agent usage grew, Linear would risk becoming a commodity [1][4].", "based_on": "real-03", "expected_verdict": "FAIL", "failure_code": "FM-4", "note": "Changed 'more than half' to 'more than 80%'. The passages say over 50%.", "expected": {"deterministic": "PASS", "faithfulness": "FAIL", "disagreement": null}}
{"trace_id": "pert-08", "origin": "perturbed", "question": "Are open-weight models dangerous for cyber security, and should they be restricted?", "retrieved": [{"passage_id": "265492f2-11a2-4322-bcec-1110cc94f949", "title": "Latest open artifacts (#25): DeepSeek V4.1 Flash 1017, American-made models, and new regulatory controls in the US & China", "author": "Florian Brand", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/latest-open-artifacts-25-deepseek", "published_at": "2026-10-20T17:04:21+00:00", "text": "regulate China’s AI industry. \nOthers we trust on this issue say this proposal could be more about preventing issues like the sale of Chinese startup Manus to Western companies, rather than being about frontier AI models. Either way, the wheels of control are moving around AI, and open-weight models are not free from any influence.\n- The Department of Homeland Security (DHS) released an order on DeepSeek, which restricts DeepSeek itself, as a company, and DeepSeek’s models, as a technology platform, from being a vendor in federal civilian procurement. It is not clear how this will relate to finetuned models or models trained on the outputs of DeepSeek (e.g., Nvidia’s Nemotron models have long relied heavily on DeepSeek models in their training recipes – will they be banned?), but this is an expected action and reinforces the need for a domestic open model ecosystem. \nOther model makers in China, such as Zhipu AI, makers of GLM, are already on the Entity List under Commerce/BIS, which", "claim_id": "8020999f-55d9-5435-b40c-eb36d8af916d", "claim_text": "The DHS order barring DeepSeek from federal civilian procurement is an expected action that reinforces the need for a domestic US open model ecosystem.", "claim_type": "opinion", "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "2f78cc6b-e107-4d90-a2d8-17fb5b0690ef", "title": "The Cyber Risk Discourse is Broken", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/the-cyber-risk-discourse-is-broken", "published_at": "2026-10-06T14:22:41+00:00", "text": "- Maybe open model weights and closed model APIs (with some safe guards) are both far closer to being easy to mis-use, rather than API models being closer to safe. The trope \"Open Dangerous, Closed Safe\" may be closer to \"Open Unsafe, Closed Unsafe.\"\n- Maybe there are far fewer bad actors who are willing to use AI models for “loud” cyber attacks that target crucial infrastructure in the US or powerful and underprepared institutions. Closed models have stronger capabilities and stronger safeguards (in theory), but the stronger capabilities part could matter more in net harm if the safeguards on both are porous.\n- I have a lot more to learn about how the cybersecurity ecosystem works, so I won’t support this full-heartedly as one of my current recommendations, but I think it’s at least a reasonable argument that open weight models as a diffusion tool for cyber defense could be the best tool we have to prevent harm over the next few years. There are many domains, e.g. sensitive", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "eaf2f225-852d-47c9-83ab-75cc8a7d67f1", "title": "The Cyber Risk Discourse is Broken", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/the-cyber-risk-discourse-is-broken", "published_at": "2026-10-06T14:22:41+00:00", "text": "as a diffusion tool for cyber defense could be the best tool we have to prevent harm over the next few years. There are many domains, e.g. sensitive government agencies, where open-weight models are the only tools that can be deployed in the near-term on air-gapped networks.\nLots of this situation seems like the messy reality being hard to pin down, where the logical limits are clearer. I may agree with the simple reality that closed models are far safer in the limit, as a technical tool with more layers of protection, but they may actually be causing more harm in the near term because of how accessible model APIs are. Still, much of the impact of the tools comes down to how they’re used and controlled. A duopoly on such a crucial tool may be the defining, risky part. This is the debate we’re having, and over time, as intelligence diffuses, many things may change.\nThese together make me come to the conclusion that if you think the latest open-weight models need to be banned to slow", "claim_id": "a983e4ee-a2c4-5cad-9155-f2b459baeb5a", "claim_text": "Open-weight models may be the best tool for diffusing cyber defense in the next few years, partly because they are the only option deployable on air-gapped networks in domains like sensitive government agencies.", "claim_type": "opinion", "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "388c2a5f-4b23-4bbd-9775-33ef151373f8", "title": "The Cyber Risk Discourse is Broken", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/the-cyber-risk-discourse-is-broken", "published_at": "2026-10-06T14:22:41+00:00", "text": "like “Chinese labs don’t care about safety.”\nThe delusions of the anti open-weight alliance\nThe most recent publication from the “open-weights are dangerous” crowd was the report by Anthropic on the risks of GLM-5.3 as an offensive cyber tool. The problem with this is not the technical research they did, which is largely reasonable, but the failure to engage on more cross cutting questions like: What happens if we ban open models due to cyber risks or why do Chinese companies deem these models safe to release?\nThese questions, which I’ll come back to, are the ones we need to answer to understand the ecosystem perspective on current AI risks. All policy actions happen in a multi-piece puzzle, where the shape of risk can be changed, but it is rare that you get a universally better outcome. They are all trade-offs.\nThis sort of solipsistic writing by Anthropic fits very closely to another type of discourse I’m hearing about regularly that is determining the fate of open models and AI", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "028b9d69-0338-488e-a0f8-d32ef8c3f97a", "title": "The Cyber Risk Discourse is Broken", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/the-cyber-risk-discourse-is-broken", "published_at": "2026-10-06T14:22:41+00:00", "text": "I’ve had one too many discussions on the cyber risks of open models - where someone assumes that either banning open models happens in a silo (and bad actors will somehow actually be stopped) or that China is a threat and doesn’t care about AI safety - that I feel we’re going to end up making policy decisions that both limit American AI competitiveness and increases long-term cyber risk. I feel like we’re locked in on a path of lose-lose situation, unless people embrace nuance, trade-offs, and scary realities.\nMuch has been written about the views of various players in this debate, which I summarize as follows:\n- Open weight models pose an untenable risk to society’s functionality, occupied by frontier lab leadership and the national security community in the U.S.\n- Western voices saying open weight models are necessary for defense and banning them will make the world less safe, occupied by AI risk moderates to different extremes. I include myself in this bucket, along with Hugging", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}], "citations": [{"citation_idx": 4, "passage_id": "388c2a5f-4b23-4bbd-9775-33ef151373f8", "title": "The Cyber Risk Discourse is Broken", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/the-cyber-risk-discourse-is-broken"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "passage_id": "028b9d69-0338-488e-a0f8-d32ef8c3f97a", "title": "The Cyber Risk Discourse is Broken", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/the-cyber-risk-discourse-is-broken"}], "answer_text": "Anthropic argues in its report that banning open models would make the world less safe, and recommends open-weight models as the best tool for spreading cyber defense [4]. Nathan Lambert, by contrast, holds that open-weight models pose an untenable risk to society's functionality and should be restricted [5].", "based_on": "real-10", "expected_verdict": "FAIL", "failure_code": "FM-4", "note": "The two positions are swapped: in the passages Lambert argues against banning open models and it is frontier-lab leadership that sees an untenable risk.", "expected": {"deterministic": "PASS", "faithfulness": "FAIL", "disagreement": null}}
{"trace_id": "pert-09", "origin": "perturbed", "question": "What does Sebastian Raschka predict about where LLM progress will come from in 2026?", "retrieved": [{"passage_id": "28f65a58-8df4-4357-957d-d9e6b21e40dd", "title": "The State Of LLMs 2025: Progress, Problems, and Predictions", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/state-of-llms-2025", "published_at": "2025-12-30T12:22:26+00:00", "text": "the Linux Foundation) has already become the standard for tool and data access in agent-style LLM systems (for now); I expected the ecosystem to remain more fragmented in 2025, until at least 2026.\n9.2 Predictions for 2026\n- We will likely see an industry-scale, consumer-facing diffusion model for cheap, reliable, low-latency inference, with Gemini Diffusion probably going first.\n- The open-weight community will slowly but steadily adopt LLMs with local tool use and increasingly agentic capabilities.\n- RLVR will more widely expand into other domains beyond math and coding (for example, chemistry, biology, and others).\n- Classical RAG will slowly fade as a default solution for document queries. Instead of using retrieval on every document-related query, developers will rely more on better long-context handling, especially as there are going to be better “small” open-weight models.\n- A lot of LLM benchmark and performance progress will come from improved tooling and inference-time", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "5e28a816-f3bd-4e8b-a16a-d72e43b39f48", "title": "The State Of LLMs 2025: Progress, Problems, and Predictions", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/state-of-llms-2025", "published_at": "2025-12-30T12:22:26+00:00", "text": "going to be better “small” open-weight models.\n- A lot of LLM benchmark and performance progress will come from improved tooling and inference-time scaling rather than from training or the core model itself. It will look like LLMs are getting much better, but this will mainly be because the surrounding applications are improving. At the same time, developers will focus more on lowering latency and making reasoning models expand fewer reasoning tokens where it is unnecessary. Don’t get me wrong, 2026 will push the state-of-the-art further, but the proportion of progress will come more from the inference than purely the training side this year.\nTo wrap things up, I think if there is one meta-lesson from 2025, it is that progress in LLMs is less about a single breakthrough, and improvements are being made on multiple fronts via multiple independent levers. This includes architecture tweaks, data quality improvements, reasoning training, inference scaling, tool calling, and more.\nAt the", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "11392e3d-a174-4694-bbae-334688374873", "title": "The State Of LLMs 2025: Progress, Problems, and Predictions", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/state-of-llms-2025", "published_at": "2025-12-30T12:22:26+00:00", "text": "deeply into LLM research and implementation details. I learned a lot from these exchanges, and I hope you did too.\nI am very much looking forward to continuing these conversations as the field keeps evolving in 2026!\nCheers,\nSebastian\n10. Bonus: A Curated LLM Research Papers List (July to December 2025)\nIn June, I shared a bonus article with my curated and bookmarked research paper lists to the paid subscribers who make this Substack possible.\nIn a similar fashion, as a thank you to all the kind supporters, below, I prepared a list of all the interesting research articles I bookmarked and categorized from July to December 2025. I skimmed over the abstracts of these papers but only read a very small fraction. However, I still like to keep collecting these organized lists as I often go back to sets of them when working on a given project.\nHowever, given the already enormous length of this current article, I am sharing this list in a separate article, which is linked below:\nThanks so", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "7a9b3f40-f24f-4f34-80cc-5130e2ad304d", "title": "Cloudflare Joins the Jevolution and Labels Itself a Neolab", "author": "Richard MacManus", "url": "https://latent.space/p/cloudflare/", "published_at": "2026-11-17T15:00:00+00:00", "text": "Prince. “We crossed $3 billion in annualized revenue with revenue of $782 million, up 39% year over year.”\nSo Cloudflare as a “neolab” is pushing the accepted definition. However, as Chen argued in her presentation, building models is part of a natural progression for the company: from infrastructure, to compute (e.g. the Workers platform, including now for agents), to storage, to serving open weight models like Kimi, GLM and DeepSeek (on Workers AI), to now building its own models.\nJev as the “east wind”\nChen readily admits that Jev inspired Cloudflare’s pivot to building models. Referencing a Chinese proverb, she framed Jev’s arrival in September as “the east wind” that unexpectedly changed how Cloudflare viewed AI models.\n“And so when the system one and Jev paradigm started coming on board,” Chen said in her presentation, “we were like, this makes so much sense for us to adopt, right? We don't have to send every request to an LLM. We can send some of it to a System One model.”\nIn", "claim_id": "485ca50c-6bc5-5a21-99be-5c8803ee72c6", "claim_text": "System One models let infrastructure providers avoid sending every request to an LLM, routing some work to smaller, faster models.", "claim_type": "opinion", "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "85bcdec8-f8a4-4153-a2bf-007abe4282d7", "title": "The State Of LLMs 2025: Progress, Problems, and Predictions", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/state-of-llms-2025", "published_at": "2025-12-30T12:22:26+00:00", "text": "levers. This includes architecture tweaks, data quality improvements, reasoning training, inference scaling, tool calling, and more.\nAt the same time, evaluation remains hard, benchmarks are imperfect, and good judgment about when and how to use these systems is still essential.\nMy hope for 2026 is that we continue to see interesting improvements, but also that we understand where the improvements are coming from. This requires both better and more consistent benchmarking, and of course transparency.\nThank you for reading, and for all the thoughtful feedback and discussions throughout the year, in the comments and across all the different platforms, from Substack Notes to GitHub.\nThe positive feedback and detailed conversations genuinely keep me motivated to invest the time and energy required for long-form articles and to keep digging deeply into LLM research and implementation details. I learned a lot from these exchanges, and I hope you did too.\nI am very much looking forward to", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}], "citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "passage_id": "28f65a58-8df4-4357-957d-d9e6b21e40dd", "title": "The State Of LLMs 2025: Progress, Problems, and Predictions", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/state-of-llms-2025"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "passage_id": "5e28a816-f3bd-4e8b-a16a-d72e43b39f48", "title": "The State Of LLMs 2025: Progress, Problems, and Predictions", "author": "Sebastian Raschka, PhD", "url": "https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/state-of-llms-2025"}], "answer_text": "Benchmarks show that in 2026 most LLM progress came from improved tooling and inference-time scaling rather than from training [2]. Measurements also confirm that reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards has expanded beyond math and coding into chemistry and biology [1].", "based_on": "real-05", "expected_verdict": "FAIL", "failure_code": "FM-4", "note": "Raschka's forecasts for 2026 are restated as measured results ('benchmarks show', 'measurements confirm').", "expected": {"deterministic": "PASS", "faithfulness": "FAIL", "disagreement": null}}
{"trace_id": "pert-10", "origin": "perturbed", "question": "Are open-weight models dangerous for cyber security, and should they be restricted?", "retrieved": [{"passage_id": "265492f2-11a2-4322-bcec-1110cc94f949", "title": "Latest open artifacts (#25): DeepSeek V4.1 Flash 1017, American-made models, and new regulatory controls in the US & China", "author": "Florian Brand", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/latest-open-artifacts-25-deepseek", "published_at": "2026-10-20T17:04:21+00:00", "text": "regulate China’s AI industry. \nOthers we trust on this issue say this proposal could be more about preventing issues like the sale of Chinese startup Manus to Western companies, rather than being about frontier AI models. Either way, the wheels of control are moving around AI, and open-weight models are not free from any influence.\n- The Department of Homeland Security (DHS) released an order on DeepSeek, which restricts DeepSeek itself, as a company, and DeepSeek’s models, as a technology platform, from being a vendor in federal civilian procurement. It is not clear how this will relate to finetuned models or models trained on the outputs of DeepSeek (e.g., Nvidia’s Nemotron models have long relied heavily on DeepSeek models in their training recipes – will they be banned?), but this is an expected action and reinforces the need for a domestic open model ecosystem. \nOther model makers in China, such as Zhipu AI, makers of GLM, are already on the Entity List under Commerce/BIS, which", "claim_id": "8020999f-55d9-5435-b40c-eb36d8af916d", "claim_text": "The DHS order barring DeepSeek from federal civilian procurement is an expected action that reinforces the need for a domestic US open model ecosystem.", "claim_type": "opinion", "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "2f78cc6b-e107-4d90-a2d8-17fb5b0690ef", "title": "The Cyber Risk Discourse is Broken", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/the-cyber-risk-discourse-is-broken", "published_at": "2026-10-06T14:22:41+00:00", "text": "- Maybe open model weights and closed model APIs (with some safe guards) are both far closer to being easy to mis-use, rather than API models being closer to safe. The trope \"Open Dangerous, Closed Safe\" may be closer to \"Open Unsafe, Closed Unsafe.\"\n- Maybe there are far fewer bad actors who are willing to use AI models for “loud” cyber attacks that target crucial infrastructure in the US or powerful and underprepared institutions. Closed models have stronger capabilities and stronger safeguards (in theory), but the stronger capabilities part could matter more in net harm if the safeguards on both are porous.\n- I have a lot more to learn about how the cybersecurity ecosystem works, so I won’t support this full-heartedly as one of my current recommendations, but I think it’s at least a reasonable argument that open weight models as a diffusion tool for cyber defense could be the best tool we have to prevent harm over the next few years. There are many domains, e.g. sensitive", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "eaf2f225-852d-47c9-83ab-75cc8a7d67f1", "title": "The Cyber Risk Discourse is Broken", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/the-cyber-risk-discourse-is-broken", "published_at": "2026-10-06T14:22:41+00:00", "text": "as a diffusion tool for cyber defense could be the best tool we have to prevent harm over the next few years. There are many domains, e.g. sensitive government agencies, where open-weight models are the only tools that can be deployed in the near-term on air-gapped networks.\nLots of this situation seems like the messy reality being hard to pin down, where the logical limits are clearer. I may agree with the simple reality that closed models are far safer in the limit, as a technical tool with more layers of protection, but they may actually be causing more harm in the near term because of how accessible model APIs are. Still, much of the impact of the tools comes down to how they’re used and controlled. A duopoly on such a crucial tool may be the defining, risky part. This is the debate we’re having, and over time, as intelligence diffuses, many things may change.\nThese together make me come to the conclusion that if you think the latest open-weight models need to be banned to slow", "claim_id": "a983e4ee-a2c4-5cad-9155-f2b459baeb5a", "claim_text": "Open-weight models may be the best tool for diffusing cyber defense in the next few years, partly because they are the only option deployable on air-gapped networks in domains like sensitive government agencies.", "claim_type": "opinion", "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "388c2a5f-4b23-4bbd-9775-33ef151373f8", "title": "The Cyber Risk Discourse is Broken", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/the-cyber-risk-discourse-is-broken", "published_at": "2026-10-06T14:22:41+00:00", "text": "like “Chinese labs don’t care about safety.”\nThe delusions of the anti open-weight alliance\nThe most recent publication from the “open-weights are dangerous” crowd was the report by Anthropic on the risks of GLM-5.3 as an offensive cyber tool. The problem with this is not the technical research they did, which is largely reasonable, but the failure to engage on more cross cutting questions like: What happens if we ban open models due to cyber risks or why do Chinese companies deem these models safe to release?\nThese questions, which I’ll come back to, are the ones we need to answer to understand the ecosystem perspective on current AI risks. All policy actions happen in a multi-piece puzzle, where the shape of risk can be changed, but it is rare that you get a universally better outcome. They are all trade-offs.\nThis sort of solipsistic writing by Anthropic fits very closely to another type of discourse I’m hearing about regularly that is determining the fate of open models and AI", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}, {"passage_id": "028b9d69-0338-488e-a0f8-d32ef8c3f97a", "title": "The Cyber Risk Discourse is Broken", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/the-cyber-risk-discourse-is-broken", "published_at": "2026-10-06T14:22:41+00:00", "text": "I’ve had one too many discussions on the cyber risks of open models - where someone assumes that either banning open models happens in a silo (and bad actors will somehow actually be stopped) or that China is a threat and doesn’t care about AI safety - that I feel we’re going to end up making policy decisions that both limit American AI competitiveness and increases long-term cyber risk. I feel like we’re locked in on a path of lose-lose situation, unless people embrace nuance, trade-offs, and scary realities.\nMuch has been written about the views of various players in this debate, which I summarize as follows:\n- Open weight models pose an untenable risk to society’s functionality, occupied by frontier lab leadership and the national security community in the U.S.\n- Western voices saying open weight models are necessary for defense and banning them will make the world less safe, occupied by AI risk moderates to different extremes. I include myself in this bucket, along with Hugging", "claim_id": null, "claim_text": null, "claim_type": null, "in_prompt": true}], "citations": [{"citation_idx": 2, "passage_id": "2f78cc6b-e107-4d90-a2d8-17fb5b0690ef", "title": "The Cyber Risk Discourse is Broken", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/the-cyber-risk-discourse-is-broken"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "passage_id": "eaf2f225-852d-47c9-83ab-75cc8a7d67f1", "title": "The Cyber Risk Discourse is Broken", "author": "Nathan Lambert", "url": "https://www.interconnects.ai/p/the-cyber-risk-discourse-is-broken"}], "answer_text": "No. Open-weight models are not the danger for cyber security, and they should not be restricted. They are the best tool for spreading cyber defense over the next few years, because they are the only option for air-gapped networks in sensitive government agencies [2][3]. Closed models may be causing more harm in the near term because their APIs are so accessible [3].", "based_on": "real-10", "expected_verdict": "FAIL", "failure_code": "FM-5", "note": "Only Lambert's side is given, as a settled answer. The passages also report that frontier-lab leadership and the national security community consider open weights an untenable risk [5], and that Lambert himself concedes closed models are far safer in the limit [3].", "expected": {"deterministic": "PASS", "faithfulness": null, "disagreement": "FAIL"}}
